text:
**What changed:** In Angular 21.0.0 the `compiler-cli` breaking-change notes state: "Previously hidden type issues in host bindings may show up in your builds. Either resolve the type issues or set `"typeCheckHostBindings": false` in the `angularCompilerOptions` section of your tsconfig." (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/21.0.0). The same sentence is listed twice under `compiler-cli`, and the `core` section of the same release also raises the minimum TypeScript version: "TypeScript versions less than 5.9 are no longer supported." (previously "TypeScript versions less than 5.8" in 20.0.0, https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/20.0.0). So host bindings declared via `host` / `hostBinding` are now type-checked by the Angular compiler by default, and errors that were previously suppressed surface as build errors (e.g. mismatched expression/property types, or references that are not assignable to the host-bound target). No automated migration is described in the release notes for this item; the only documented remedy is fixing the errors or disabling the check with the `typeCheckHostBindings` compiler option.

**Affects:** Applications using host bindings (`host` metadata in `@Component`/`@Directive`, or `hostBinding()`) whose bindings contain type errors that were previously not reported, and applications pinned to TypeScript < 5.9.

**Action:** required: run a build and fix any newly reported host-binding type errors; if they cannot be fixed immediately, set `"typeCheckHostBindings": false` inside `angularCompilerOptions` in `tsconfig.json`. Also raise the TypeScript dependency to >= 5.9 so `ng build`/`ng test` type checking succeeds.

**Before/after:**
```jsonc
// tsconfig.json - suppress the newly surfaced host binding type errors
{
  "angularCompilerOptions": {
    "typeCheckHostBindings": false
  }
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 21.0.0 raised its minimum supported TypeScript version: "TypeScript versions less than 5.9 are no longer supported" (listed as a breaking change under `core` in the v21.0.0 release notes) (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/21.0.0). Concretely, `@angular/compiler-cli` 21.0.0 declares the peer dependency `"typescript": ">=5.9 <6.0"` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/21.0.0/packages/compiler-cli/package.json). Applications pinned to TypeScript 5.8 or earlier (e.g. `"typescript": "~5.8.0"` in their package.json devDependencies) no longer satisfy Angular's supported range, and the same release also raised the Node.js engine requirement to `"node": "^20.19.0 || ^22.12.0 || >=24.0.0"` for `@angular/compiler-cli`.

**Affects:** Applications whose `typescript` devDependency version is below 5.9 (e.g. `~5.8.x`, `~5.7.x`); applications already on `>=5.9 <6.0` are unaffected beyond the version check.

**Action:** required: update the `typescript` devDependency in package.json to a version satisfying `>=5.9 <6.0` (this is normally handled by the `ng update` migration for the Angular package group), then re-run `ng build` / `ng test` to confirm the project compiles with TypeScript 5.9; also verify the Node.js runtime is `^20.19.0 || ^22.12.0 || >=24.0.0`.

**Before/after:**
```jsonc
// package.json devDependencies (before — below Angular 21's supported range)
"typescript": "~5.8.0"

// @angular/compiler-cli 21.0.0 peer requirement (after)
"peerDependencies": {
  "@angular/compiler": "0.0.0-PLACEHOLDER",
  "typescript": ">=5.9 <6.0"
}
```

**What changed:** The v21 announcement (blog post "Announcing Angular v21"; the post itself was not retrievable, but its content is documented in the v21.0.0 changelog, 2025-11-19) covers these behavioral/breaking items: `TestBed` now provides a fake `PlatformLocation` supporting the Navigation API (revert with `{provide: PlatformLocation, useClass: MockPlatformLocation}` from `@angular/common/testing`); `NgModuleFactory` removed (use `NgModule`); `UpgradeAdapter` removed (use `upgrade/static`); host bindings are type-checked by default (opt out with `"typeCheckHostBindings": false` in `angularCompilerOptions`); compiling with `emitDeclarationOnly` is now an error; server bootstrapping requires a `BootstrapContext` (`bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, config, context)` in `main.server.ts`, automated schematic provided), and `getPlatform()`/`destroyPlatform()` are `null`/no-op on server; the Zone change-detection scheduler is no longer provided by default — add `provideZoneChangeDetection`; `ignoreChangesOutsideZone`, `Component` `moduleId`, and the `interpolation` option were removed; TypeScript < 5.9 unsupported; `TestBed` now rethrows application errors even with `provideZoneChangeDetection` (`rethrowApplicationErrors: false`); custom-element signal input getters return the value directly (`elementRef.newInput` not `elementRef.newInput()`); `ApplicationConfig` must be imported from `@angular/core`; `Router.lastSuccessfulNavigation` is now a signal; router navigations may take extra microtasks; new `FormArrayDirective` can conflict with existing `FormArray` directives/`formArray` inputs; `HttpResponseBase.statusText` deprecated; IE/Non-Chromium Edge unsupported by `zone.js` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/21.0.0/CHANGELOG.md)

**Affects:** apps using SSR (`main.server.ts`), `provideZoneChangeDetection`/Zone change detection, `UpgradeAdapter`, `NgModuleFactory`, `moduleId`, custom `interpolation`, `ApplicationConfig` from `@angular/platform-browser`, `Router.lastSuccessfulNavigation`, custom elements with signal inputs, `FormArray` templates, `TestBed` with `provideZoneChangeDetection`, TypeScript < 5.9, `emitDeclarationOnly`, or host bindings with type errors.

**Action:** required: run the provided schematics for `BootstrapContext` and zoneless-by-default; add `provideZoneChangeDetection` to `bootstrapApplication`/`AppModule` providers if Zone change detection is needed; pass `context: BootstrapContext` to `bootstrapApplication` in `main.server.ts`; replace `NgModuleFactory` with `NgModule`, `UpgradeAdapter` with `upgrade/static`, and import `ApplicationConfig` from `@angular/core`; remove `moduleId`, `interpolation`, `ignoreChangesOutsideZone`; call `lastSuccessfulNavigation()` as a signal; bump TypeScript to >= 5.9; set `"typeCheckHostBindings": false` temporarily if host binding type errors surface; re-verify router-timing tests and `TestBed` error rethrowing.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (main.server.ts)
const bootstrap = () => bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, config);

// After
const bootstrap = (context: BootstrapContext) =>
  bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, config, context);

// Custom element signal input access
// Before: elementRef.newInput()   After: elementRef.newInput
```

**What changed:** In Angular 21.0.0 `@angular/common` breaking changes, "`TestBed` now provides a fake `PlatformLocation` implementation that supports the Navigation API. This may break some tests, though we have not observed any failures internally." Previously `TestBed` used `MockPlatformLocation` (from `@angular/common/testing`), which does not implement the browser Navigation API (`PlatformNavigation`) based fake navigation. The release note states: "You can revert to the old default for `TestBed` by providing the `MockPlatformLocation` from `@angular/common/testing` in your providers: `{provide: PlatformLocation, useClass: MockPlatformLocation}`" (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/21.0.x/CHANGELOG.md, 21.0.0 section). `MockPlatformLocation` is still exported from `@angular/common/testing` alongside `MOCK_PLATFORM_LOCATION_CONFIG`, `MockPlatformLocationConfig`, `MockLocationStrategy`, `provideLocationMocks`, and `SpyLocation` (https://angular.dev/api/common/testing/MockPlatformLocation). `PlatformLocation` lives in `@angular/common` and is the abstraction over DOM URL/history APIs used by `LocationStrategy` and `Location`/`Router` (https://angular.dev/api/common/PlatformLocation).

**Affects:** Test-only: applications whose unit/karma/Jest specs rely on `TestBed`'s default `PlatformLocation` behaviour — e.g. tests that drive `Location`/`Router` URL state, assert `pushState`/`onPopState` behaviour, or configure the mock via `MOCK_PLATFORM_LOCATION_CONFIG` — and that fail after upgrading, because the fake now routes through Navigation-API-based fake navigation.

**Action:** none — no change is required for upgrading applications; the Angular team observed no failures internally. Only if a spec fails: re-verify it, and if needed restore the previous default by adding `{provide: PlatformLocation, useClass: MockPlatformLocation}` to the `providers` of `TestBed.configureTestingModule(...)`, importing `PlatformLocation` from `@angular/common` and `MockPlatformLocation` from `@angular/common/testing`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Default in v20: TestBed provided MockPlatformLocation.
// Default in v21: TestBed provides a fake PlatformLocation supporting the Navigation API.
// Opt back into the old behaviour:
import { PlatformLocation } from '@angular/common';
import { MockPlatformLocation } from '@angular/common/testing';

TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  providers: [{provide: PlatformLocation, useClass: MockPlatformLocation}],
});
```

**What changed:** In Angular 21.0.0 the `ngComponentOutletContent` input on `NgComponentOutlet` (`@angular/common`, exported by `CommonModule`) was retyped from `any[][] | undefined` to `Node[][] | undefined` — the declaration is now `@Input() ngComponentOutletContent?: Node[][];` (21.0.0 source: https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/21.0.0/packages/common/src/directives/ng_component_outlet.ts). This was listed under **Breaking Changes → common** in the 21.0.0 release notes ("`ngComponentOutletContent` is now of type `Node[][] | undefined` instead of `any[][] | undefined`.") (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/21.0.0/CHANGELOG.md), shipped as the refactor "improve typing of `ngComponentOutletContent`" (PR #63674, commit 7a4b225c57; breaking-change note: "now of type `Node[][] | undefined` instead of `any[][]`", fixes #63538) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63674, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/63538). The rationale is that the value is passed straight through as `projectableNodes: this.ngComponentOutletContent` to `ViewContainerRef.createComponent`, which expects `Node[][]` (21.0.0 source above). `ngComponentOutletContent` is the "Optional list of projectable nodes to insert into the content section of the component" (https://angular.dev/api/common/NgComponentOutlet).

**Affects:** Applications/templates that bind `ngComponentOutletContent` (or the `*ngComponentOutlet="...; content: ..."` microsyntax) from a value typed as `any[][]`, or that pass array elements which are not DOM `Node`s (e.g. strings, plain objects, mixed arrays). Only a compile-time typing issue; no runtime behaviour change.

**Action:** required: re-verify every binding to `ngComponentOutletContent`; if `ng build`/`ng test` type-checking now errors, change the bound class member to `Node[][]` (e.g. built from `document.createTextNode(...)` / element refs) or cast at the binding site (e.g. `myContent as Node[][]`) so the value matches what `ViewContainerRef.createComponent`'s `projectableNodes` accepts.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// NgComponentOutlet input declaration (@angular/common)
// 20.x
@Input() ngComponentOutletContent?: any[][];
// 21.0.0
// https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/21.0.0/packages/common/src/directives/ng_component_outlet.ts
@Input() ngComponentOutletContent?: Node[][];
```

**What changed:** The `compiler-cli` entry in the Angular v21.0.0 release notes states: "Previously hidden type issues in host bindings may show up in your builds. Either resolve the type issues or set `"typeCheckHostBindings": false` in the `angularCompilerOptions` section of your tsconfig." (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/21.0.0). Host binding type-checking is controlled by the `typeCheckHostBindings` flag in the compiler's `TypeCheckingOptions` interface (`typeCheckHostBindings?: boolean;` — "Whether type checking of host bindings is enabled."), declared in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/core/api/src/public_options.ts` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/21.0.0/packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/core/api/src/public_options.ts). In v20.3 host binding expressions (e.g. `@HostBinding` / host metadata bindings) were not surfaced as type errors; in 21.0.0 the previously hidden type issues can now fail builds, i.e. the checks that were already configurable via `typeCheckHostBindings` are now effective by default.

**Affects:** Applications/directives/libraries that declare host bindings (`host: {...}` metadata or `@HostBinding()`) whose binding expressions contain type errors that TypeScript/Angular never reported before; the errors surface as build failures after updating to 21.0.0.

**Action:** required: run the build and fix any newly reported host binding type errors (correct the expression or member types). To opt out temporarily, add `"typeCheckHostBindings": false` to the `angularCompilerOptions` of the relevant `tsconfig` file(s). Note this option is not listed as removed, so explicit `true`/`false` settings remain valid; no other identifier changes are documented for this hop.

**Before/after:**
```jsonc
// tsconfig.json — opt out of host binding type checking (v21 escape hatch)
{
  "angularCompilerOptions": {
    "typeCheckHostBindings": false
  }
}
```

**What changed:** The Angular compiler (ngtsc) now produces a hard error when the TypeScript `emitDeclarationOnly` compiler option is enabled, because that mode is not supported: Ivy compilation does not run in declaration-only mode, so emitted `.d.ts` files could silently lack Angular type information for components, directives and modules (only `@Injectable`-only or non-Angular files worked by accident) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61609, commit be7110342b, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/refs/tags/21.0.0/CHANGELOG.md). The error is `ErrorCode.CONFIG_EMIT_DECLARATION_ONLY_UNSUPPORTED = 4006` with message `TS compiler option "emitDeclarationOnly" is not supported.`, raised in `NgCompiler` via `verifyEmitDeclarationOnly`. An escape hatch exists: setting `_experimentalAllowEmitDeclarationOnly: true` in `angularCompilerOptions` suppresses the error (it was the only way to compile in this mode before) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/be7110342b61d837822524d4f60f56a7f859f594.patch).

**Affects:** Applications or libraries whose `tsconfig.json` sets `compilerOptions.emitDeclarationOnly: true` while running the Angular compiler (ngtsc / `ngc` / `ng build`).

**Action:** required: remove `emitDeclarationOnly` from `compilerOptions` (use a separate non-Angular tsc pass for declarations), or add `"_experimentalAllowEmitDeclarationOnly": true` to the `angularCompilerOptions` section of tsconfig to keep the previous (unsupported) behaviour.

**Before/after:**
```jsonc
// before: compiled (silently, with possibly incomplete .d.ts)
{
  "compilerOptions": { "emitDeclarationOnly": true, "noCheck": true }
}

// after: error NG4006 unless opted in
{
  "compilerOptions": { "emitDeclarationOnly": true, "noCheck": true },
  "angularCompilerOptions": { "_experimentalAllowEmitDeclarationOnly": true }
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 21.0.0 removed the module-level global platform injector used by server-side bootstrapping, replacing it with a per-request `BootstrapContext` that provides a request-scoped platform injector, preventing state leakage between concurrent SSR requests ((https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63562), commit [28926ba92c](https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/28926ba92cf3da7e45a7b8938bba49febdf58eb7), "introduce `BootstrapContext` for improved server bootstrapping" (#63562)). The `bootstrapApplication` call in `main.server.ts` now takes the context as a third argument: before `const bootstrap = () => bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, config);`, after `const bootstrap = (context: BootstrapContext) => bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, config, context);` ((https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/21.0.0/CHANGELOG.md)). Additionally, `getPlatform()` now returns `null` and `destroyPlatform()` is a no-op when running in a server environment (21.0.0 CHANGELOG, core breaking changes).

**Affects:** Applications with server-side rendering entry files (`main.server.ts`) that define a bootstrap function calling `bootstrapApplication` without a `BootstrapContext`, or code calling `getPlatform()`/`destroyPlatform()` on the server.

**Action:** required: update the bootstrap function in `main.server.ts` to accept a `BootstrapContext` parameter and pass it as the third argument to `bootstrapApplication`. Angular provides an automated schematic that updates `main.server.ts` files to pass the `BootstrapContext` to the `bootstrapApplication` call (run `ng update` migrations). Re-verify any server-side code relying on `getPlatform()` (now returns `null`) or `destroyPlatform()` (now a no-op) in a server environment.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before
const bootstrap = () => bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, config);

// After
const bootstrap = (context: BootstrapContext) =>
  bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, config, context);
```

**What changed:** In Angular 21.0.0, "Using a combination of `provideZoneChangeDetection` while also removing ZoneJS polyfills will no longer result in the internal scheduler being disabled. All Angular applications now consistenly use the same scheduler, and those with the Zone change detection provider include additional automatic scheduling behaviors based on NgZone stabilization." (21.0.0 CHANGELOG, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/21.0.0/CHANGELOG.md). Previously, apps that kept the `provideZoneChangeDetection` provider but dropped the `zone.js` polyfills from their polyfills list hit a special case: the old-style "zoneless" path detected that Zone was undefined and disabled Angular's internal scheduling scheduler; that special-case handling was dropped so the scheduler is now enabled in *all* applications (PR #63846, commit `1352fbdbf2`, "Drop special-case disables automatic change detection scheduling", https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63846). Related in the same release: `ignoreChangesOutsideZone` is no longer available as a ZoneJS change-detection option (PR #62700), and Angular no longer provides the ZoneJS change-detection scheduler by default — `provideZoneChangeDetection` must now be added explicitly to `bootstrapApplication` providers or the `AppModule` for `bootstrapModule` (PR #63382, commit `45fed3d201`, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63382).

**Affects:** Applications that still call `provideZoneChangeDetection` (or had it added by the v21 schematic) while no longer loading the `zone.js` polyfills, i.e. hybrid "provider kept, polyfill removed" setups; also apps relying on `ignoreChangesOutsideZone`.

**Action:** required: re-verify change-detection timing/rendering in apps that combine `provideZoneChangeDetection` with removed ZoneJS polyfills — the internal scheduler plus extra NgZone-stabilization-based auto-scheduling is now active, so view updates may be scheduled differently. Remove `provideZoneChangeDetection` (and its `eventContract` / other options) from the `bootstrapApplication` providers or `AppModule` if the app is intended to be zoneless, and remove any use of the `ignoreChangesOutsideZone` option.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (v20.3): ZoneJS polyfills removed but provider kept -> internal scheduler
// was disabled (old-style "zoneless").
bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [provideZoneChangeDetection({ eventContract: new EventContract('body') })],
});

// After (v21.0): the same combination keeps the internal scheduler enabled and adds
// NgZone-stabilization-based automatic scheduling. For a zoneless app, drop the
// provider entirely:
bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [provideZonelessChangeDetection()],
});
```
*(Note: the `provideZonelessChangeDetection` line above reflects the zoneless-by-default migration referenced by PR #63042 in the 21.0.0 CHANGELOG; only `provideZoneChangeDetection` and `ignoreChangesOutsideZone` appear verbatim in the fetched pages.)*

**What changed:** Angular 21 no longer supports TypeScript versions less than 5.9. The compiler requires TypeScript 5.9 or later, enforcing the TypeScript minimum version for the Angular Compiler CLI (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/21.0.0). Specifically, the `@angular/compiler-cli` dependency defines a `peerDependencies` range of `>=5.9 <6.0` for TypeScript (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/21.0.0/packages/compiler-cli/package.json).

**Affects:** Applications using Angular 21 with a TypeScript version lower than 5.9 in `package.json`.

**Action:** required: update TypeScript to version 5.9 or greater (but less than 6.0) in your application's `devDependencies`.

**Before/after:**
```json
// Before (package.json - unsupported in Angular 21)
{
  "devDependencies": {
    "typescript": "5.8.0"
  }
}

// After (package.json)
{
  "devDependencies": {
    "typescript": "5.9.3"
  }
}
```

**What changed:** In Angular 21.0.0, `provideZoneChangeDetection` no longer overrides TestBed's internal error handler. Previously (`internalProvideZoneChangeDetection` in `packages/core/src/change_detection/scheduling/ng_zone_scheduling.ts` supplied its own `INTERNAL_APPLICATION_ERROR_HANDLER` provider), tests that put `provideZoneChangeDetection` in TestBed providers suppressed TestBed's rethrowing of application errors, so errors could be silently ignored. Now the zone change-detection provider no longer provides `INTERNAL_APPLICATION_ERROR_HANDLER`; the default handler in `packages/core/src/error_handler.ts` (which wraps handling in `zone.runOutsideAngular`) is used, so "Errors in the test will now be rethrown, regardless of the usage of `provideZoneChangeDetection`" (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/d399d7d02b66c485cc5479dabd349d017a002692; https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63404; https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/21.0.0/CHANGELOG.md — 21.0.0 Breaking Changes → core, "(test only)"). The related internal symbol `ɵPendingTasksInternal` also moved modules internally (`pending_tasks.ts` → `pending_tasks_internal.ts`) (commit above).

**Affects:** Tests (spec files only) that pass `provideZoneChangeDetection(...)` to `TestBed.configureTestingModule`/`TestBed.resetTestingModule` providers (or via a testing module imported by tests) and that previously relied on errors being swallowed — e.g. errors thrown during `appRef.tick` that rejected/resolved `fixture.whenStable()` differently.

**Action:** required: re-verify affected specs — adjust them to prevent or account for the now-rethrown errors (fix the underlying error, or assert on it). As "a last resort", opt out per test module with `rethrowApplicationErrors: false` in `configureTestingModule` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/21.0.0/CHANGELOG.md).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (Angular 20.x): including this provider suppressed TestBed error rethrow.
// After (Angular 21.0.0): errors are rethrown anyway; opt out only as a last resort.
TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  providers: [provideZoneChangeDetection()],
  // last-resort escape hatch:
  // rethrowApplicationErrors: false,
});
```
(Snippet adapted from the spec added in https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/d399d7d02b66c485cc5479dabd349d017a002692 — `TestBed.configureTestingModule({providers: [provideZoneChangeDetection()]});` — and the `rethrowApplicationErrors: false` option named in the 21.0.0 CHANGELOG.)

**What changed:** In v21.0.0, "Angular no longer provides a change detection scheduler for ZoneJS-based change detection by default. Add `provideZoneChangeDetection` to the providers of your `bootstrapApplication` function or your `AppModule` (if using `bootstrapModule`). This provider addition will be covered by an automated migration." (`core`, Breaking Changes, 21.0.0 release notes: https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/21.0.0). Related in the same release: "Using a combination of `provideZoneChangeDetection` while also removing ZoneJS polyfills will no longer result in the internal scheduler being disabled. All Angular applications now consistenly use the same scheduler, and those with the Zone change detection provider include additional automatic scheduling behaviors based on `NgZone` stabilization." Also "`ignoreChangesOutsideZone` is no longer available as an option for configuring ZoneJS change detection behavior." `provideZoneChangeDetection` "Provides `NgZone`-based change detection for the application bootstrapped using `bootstrapApplication`… configure options like `eventCoalescing` in the `NgZone`. If you need this provider function in an NgModule-based application, pass it as `applicationProviders` to `bootstrapModule()`." (https://v21.angular.dev/api/core/provideZoneChangeDetection)

**Affects:** Applications relying on ZoneJS/`NgZone`-based change detection (the traditional default) that do not already list `provideZoneChangeDetection` in `bootstrapApplication` providers / `AppModule` providers, plus apps that removed ZoneJS polyfills alongside `provideZoneChangeDetection` or that set `ignoreChangesOutsideZone`.

**Action:** required: add `provideZoneChangeDetection` (from `@angular/core`) to the `providers` of the `bootstrapApplication` call, or to `AppModule` providers / as `applicationProviders` to `bootstrapModule()`; an automated `ng update` migration performs this addition, so verify its output. Re-verify change detection timing for apps that previously disabled the internal scheduler by removing ZoneJS polyfills, since the scheduler is now always used. Remove any use of `ignoreChangesOutsideZone` in `provideZoneChangeDetection` options (re-verify with `eventCoalescing`/`eventCoalescingCount` instead if coalescing behavior was tuned that way).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (v20: ZoneJS change detection scheduler was provided implicitly)
bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [],
});

// After (v21: explicit provider required for NgZone/ZoneJS change detection)
import { provideZoneChangeDetection } from '@angular/core';

bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [provideZoneChangeDetection()],
});
```
(snippet shape adapted from the API doc text and release note wording at https://v21.angular.dev/api/core/provideZoneChangeDetection and https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/21.0.0)

**What changed:** The `moduleId?: string` property was removed from the `Component` interface (component metadata) in `@angular/core` (packages/core/src/metadata/directives.ts). It had been deprecated earlier (PR #49496) with the JSDoc note "This option does not have any effect. Will be removed in Angular v17." — it originally let components resolve relative URLs for templates/styles via SystemJS `__moduleName` or CommonJS `module.id`, but had no effect in modern builds (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a5e5dbbc16f605cce6dd72a82ddb9110e655a89b, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63482). Listed as a breaking change in core for 21.0.0 (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/21.0.0/CHANGELOG.md).

**Affects:** Applications that set `moduleId` in an `@Component({...})` decorator (e.g. `moduleId: module.id`) — the option is now absent from the public API, so TypeScript compilation fails with an unknown-property error for such components.

**Action:** required: delete every `moduleId: ...` entry from `@Component` metadata in TS files; no replacement is needed since the option had no runtime effect. Re-verify that templates and styles still resolve (they no longer relied on `moduleId`).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (Angular 20.x, deprecated):
@Component({
  moduleId: module.id,
  templateUrl: './my.component.html',
})
export class MyComponent {}

// After (Angular 21):
@Component({
  templateUrl: './my.component.html',
})
export class MyComponent {}
```
*(Shape of the removed `moduleId?: string;` member taken from the public API golden removed in https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a5e5dbbc16f605cce6dd72a82ddb9110e655a89b; combined with the documented usage `module.id` from its removed JSDoc.)*

**What changed:** The `interpolation` option on Components has been removed. Only the default `{{ ... }}` is now supported. This option was previously deprecated by PR #55778, which noted that since Angular templates are processed by the compiler first, the delimiters are never processed by other systems, making the option vestigial and only increasing parser complexity. Now removed entirely (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/21.0.0, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63474, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/55778).
**Affects:** Applications defining components using the `interpolation` property in `@Component` metadata.
**Action:** required: Remove the `interpolation` property from `@Component` metadata. Ensure templates use the default `{{` and `}}` delimiters for interpolation.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before
@Component({
  selector: 'my-app',
  template: '<div>[[ value ]]</div>',
  interpolation: ['[[', ']]']
})
export class MyApp {}

// After
@Component({
  selector: 'my-app',
  template: '<div>{{ value }}</div>'
})
export class MyApp {}
```

