**Affects:** Applications/templates containing `@defer` blocks with misconfigured triggers — e.g. `on immediate` combined with other main triggers or with any `prefetch on`, prefetch `timer(...)` delays not earlier than the main `timer(...)`, or a prefetch identical to the main trigger. New build/template-check or IDE output for previously-silent blocks.

**Action:** none (informational/behavioural: newly flagged templates should be reviewed; remove the redundant prefetch or adjust trigger ordering/delays if the diagnostic surfaces in your build).

**Before/after:**
```angular-html
<!-- Flagged in 21.0 (prefetch never runs before render / no benefit) -->
@defer (on timer(100ms); prefetch on timer(3000ms)) { <my-cmp /> }
@defer (on viewport; prefetch on viewport) { <my-cmp /> }
@defer (on immediate; prefetch on idle) { <my-cmp /> }
```

**What changed:** Angular 21 enables type checking of host bindings by default. Type checking of host bindings was added in v20 behind the opt-in `typeCheckHostBindings` `angularCompilerOptions` flag; in 21.0.0 the flag now defaults to `true`, so the compiler "enables type checking of expressions in the host object literal and `@HostBinding` / `@HostListener` decorators of components and directives" (https://angular.dev/reference/configs/angular-compiler-options — "Default is `true`"). Previously hidden type issues in host bindings can now surface as build errors (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0571b335b9b11459b73a19679671eae97fbe1683, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63654, https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/21.0.0).

**Affects:** Applications with components/directives using `host: {...}` object literals, `@HostBinding`, or `@HostListener` whose expressions contain type errors that went unnoticed before upgrading.

**Action:** required: build with 21 and fix any new host-binding diagnostics; if they cannot be fixed immediately, opt out by setting `"typeCheckHostBindings": false` in the `angularCompilerOptions` section of `tsconfig.json` (per the commit/PR; the option can also be inherited via `extends`).

**Before/after:**
```jsonc
// tsconfig.json — opt out of the new default if needed
{
  "angularCompilerOptions": {
    "typeCheckHostBindings": false
  }
}
```

**What changed:** `compiler-cli` now emits metadata for undecorated, initializer-based members (signal `input()`, `output()`, `model()`, and signal queries `viewChild()`/`viewChildren()`/`contentChild()`/`contentChildren()`) into the `setClassMetadata` calls it generates, in addition to decorated members. Previously `setClassMetadata` captured only decorated members, so when `TestBed.overrideComponent` re-compiled a class from `setClassMetadata` info, the initializer metadata was wiped out and signal inputs/outputs/queries stopped working — most visible in a JIT/AOT mix where JIT transforms aren't applied (issue #57944, investigation in PR #57668). Internally a new `UndecoratedMetadataExtractor` parameter was added to `extractClassMetadata`, wired in via `getDirectiveUndecoratedMetadataExtractor` in the component/directive decorator handlers (`packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/annotations/...`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ab98b2425f4c4cb59927aa686818ecee99e634c7, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63957, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/57944). The generated `propDecorators` entries now include e.g. `{ type: i0.Input, args: [{ isSignal: true, alias: "counter", required: false }] }` and `{ type: i0.Output, args: ["counterChange"] }`, and member names containing dashes are now emitted with double quotes instead of single quotes (commit patch).

**Affects:** applications/components that use `TestBed.overrideComponent` (or other `TestBed.override*`) on classes using `input()`, `output()`, `model()`, or signal queries, especially in mixed JIT/AOT compilation setups; also library consumers relying on the emitted `ɵɵngDeclareClassMetadata` / `setClassMetadata` output shape (GOLDEN_PARTIAL output changes).

**Action:** none — no application code, configuration or dependency changes; this is an internal compiler-cli fix. Re-verify tests that use `TestBed.overrideComponent` together with signal inputs/outputs/queries, and tests that assert on generated `setClassMetadata`/`propDecorators` output (note quoting of dashed member names changed from `'has-dashes-in-name'` to `"has-dashes-in-name"`).

**Before/after:**
```javascript
// Before (model() members had no propDecorators):
i0.ɵɵngDeclareClassMetadata({ ..., type: TestDir, decorators: [{
    type: Directive,
    args: [{}]
}] });

// After (undecorated initializer APIs are captured):
i0.ɵɵngDeclareClassMetadata({ ..., type: TestDir, decorators: [{
    type: Directive,
    args: [{}]
}], propDecorators: { counter: [{ type: i0.Input, args: [{ isSignal: true, alias: "counter", required: false }] }, { type: i0.Output, args: ["counterChange"] }], name: [{ type: i0.Input, args: [{ isSignal: true, alias: "name", required: true }] }, { type: i0.Output, args: ["nameChange"] }] } });
```
(https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ab98b2425f4c4cb59927aa686818ecee99e634c7)

**What changed:** The Angular compiler (`@angular/compiler-cli` via `ng build` / `ngtsc` `NgCompiler`) now emits a configuration diagnostic `ErrorCode.CONFIG_EMIT_DECLARATION_ONLY_UNSUPPORTED` (code 4006) with the message `TS compiler option "emitDeclarationOnly" is not supported.` when the TypeScript `emitDeclarationOnly` compiler option is enabled, because the compiler relies on Ivy compilation which does not run in declaration-only mode (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/be7110342b61d837822524d4f60f56a7f859f594, https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/61609.diff). Previously compilation either silently succeeded with incomplete `.d.ts` output for components, directives and modules, or failed only incidentally. An experimental escape hatch `angularCompilerOptions._experimentalAllowEmitDeclarationOnly` (set to `true`) suppresses the error (https://patch-diff.githubusercontent.com/raw/angular/angular/pull/61609.diff).

**Affects:** Applications or libraries whose `tsconfig.json` sets `"emitDeclarationOnly": true` in `compilerOptions` (typically declaration-only builds run through the Angular compiler / `ngc`).

**Action:** required: Remove or disable `emitDeclarationOnly` in `tsconfig.json`, or explicitly allow it by setting `angularCompilerOptions._experimentalAllowEmitDeclarationOnly: true` in the Angular compiler options. Re-verify emitted type declarations for components, directives and modules.

**Before/after:**
```json
// tsconfig.json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "emitDeclarationOnly": true,
    "noCheck": true
  },
  "angularCompilerOptions": {
    "_experimentalAllowEmitDeclarationOnly": true
  }
}
```

**What changed:** The Angular template type-checker (`compiler-cli`) now infers the model type of a *custom form field control* used together with the `Field` directive from `@angular/forms/signals` on the same element. Before, only DOM nodes (e.g. `<input>`, `<textarea>`, `<select>`) had their expected type inferred for `[field]`; custom controls fell back to an "unsupported" type (there was a `// TODO: check for custom control.`). Now, in `TcbFieldDirectiveTypeOp`, a directive matching the element is detected as a custom field control when it has a **signal** `value` input with a `valueChange` output (a `FormValueControl`), or a **signal** `checked` input with a `checkedChange` output (a `FormCheckboxControl`) — e.g. `readonly value = model<User>(...)`. The expected type for `[field]` is then computed as `ExtractFormControlValue<CustomControlDirective>` (via `R3Identifiers.ExtractFormControlValue`), so a mismatched `form(...)` model produces a real type error like `Type 'FieldTree<{ name: string; }, string | number>' is not assignable to type 'FieldTree<User, string | number>'` (previously these mismatches went undetected). Note: `Field`-named directives not from `@angular/forms/signals` are still treated as regular directives (packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/src/type_check_block.ts) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/948e2f4f0182f6e328200899692f41c904a2e8b5.patch).

**Affects:** Applications using signal forms (`@angular/forms/signals` `Field` directive) with a custom control component that has a signal/model input `value` + `valueChange` output or `checked` + `checkedChange` output, bound via `[field]` on the same element. Can surface new template type-check errors in previously "passing" code where the form model type didn't match the control's model type.

**Action:** none for correct code — no API changes. After upgrading, re-run template type-checking (`ng build` / `ng test`); fix any newly reported `[field]` assignment errors by aligning the `form(signal(...))` model type with the custom control's `model()` type (or removing the erroneous binding).

**Before/after:**
```html
<!-- before: no type error (custom control type was not inferred) -->
<!-- after: error: Type 'FieldTree<{ name: string; }, string | number>' is not assignable to
     type 'FieldTree<User, string | number>' -->
<user-control [field]="f"/>
```
```ts
// custom control (from the fix's test case)
@Component({selector: 'user-control', template: ''})
export class UserControl implements FormValueControl<User> {
  readonly value = model<User>({firstName: 'Frodo', lastName: 'Baggins'});
}
export class Comp {
  f = form(signal({name: 'Bilbo'})); // mismatch now caught
}
```
(https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/948e2f4f0182f6e328200899692f41c904a2e8b5.patch)

**What changed:** The Angular template type-checker (`compiler-cli`) now infers the generic model/view type of the signal-forms `Field` directive from `@angular/forms/signals` when it is applied to a native element, instead of treating it as an untyped/`any` directive (new `TcbFieldDirectiveTypeOp` in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/src/type_check_block.ts`). The inferred "expected" type of `[field]` is `() => FieldState<T, string | number>` where `T` comes from the element: `<textarea>` and `<select>` → `string`; `<input>` without a `type` or with an unrecognized/`text`/`radio` type → `string`; `type="checkbox"` → `boolean`; `type="number"`, `range`, `datetime-local` → `string | number`; `type="date"`, `month`, `time`, `week` → `string | number | Date | null`; non-element (host/component) usage → `unknown` (`any`-like fallback, custom controls not yet handled) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/96cb0cffda55516e01613958d1268872f1070722, patch file).

**Affects:** Applications using the signal-forms `Field` directive (`@angular/forms/signals`) on native `<input>`, `<textarea>` or `<select>` elements in templates, with `strictTemplates` type checking enabled. Previously mismatched model types were not reported; now they can produce new template type-check errors such as `Type '() => FieldState<number, string | number>' is not assignable to type '() => FieldState<string, string | number>'.`

**Action:** required: run `ng build`/`ng test` (template type checking) and fix newly reported type errors by aligning the model signal's type with the element's inferred view type, for example wrap a numeric model used on a plain `<input>`/`<textarea>`/`<select>` so the field type is `string`, or set `type="number"`/`checkbox` on the `<input>` to match a `number`/`boolean` model. Fields bound on custom components (non-native elements) still fall back to `unknown` and are not checked (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/96cb0cffda55516e01613958d1268872f1070722).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before 21.0: no type error reported for the mismatch.
// After 21.0 (strictTemplates): diagnostic
// "Type '() => FieldState<number, string | number>' is not assignable
//  to type '() => FieldState<string, string | number>'."
@Component({
  template: '<input [field]="f"/>',
  imports: [Field]
})
export class Comp {
  f = form(signal(0));
}
```

The commit page and patch give the full picture: it's an internal `compiler-cli` change in `type_check_block.ts` (`isFieldDirective`), no public API changes.

**What changed:** The Angular template type-checking compiler's internal detection of `Field` directives (from the signals forms API) was made more robust. Previously, in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/src/type_check_block.ts`, `isFieldDirective` matched only when the directive's name was `Field` **and** its `meta.ref.bestGuessOwningModule?.specifier` was `@angular/forms/signals` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/71ab11ccf0f0daaffb49779d5f90b9e2da76dbd5.patch). Now, after checking `meta.name !== 'Field'`, the `@angular/forms/signals` module specifier is only a "fast path"; if it doesn't match, the compiler additionally checks whether the directive class declaration contains a computed property member named `ɵCONTROL` (i.e. `ts.isComputedPropertyName(member.name)` with identifier text `ɵCONTROL`), and treats such directives as `Field` directives too (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/71ab11ccf0f0daaffb49779d5f90b9e2da76dbd5.patch).

**Affects:** Effectively none for applications — the commit message states the looser check was needed "to cover our own tests"; user code importing `Field` from `@angular/forms/signals` still hits the fast path. Only code defining a custom `Field`-named directive with a `ɵCONTROL` member outside `@angular/forms/signals` would newly be treated as a signal-forms `Field` during template type checking.

**Action:** none

```typescript
// new isFieldDirective logic (compiler-cli internal)
if (meta.name !== 'Field') {
  return false;
}

// Fast path, relevant for all external users.
if (meta.ref.bestGuessOwningModule?.specifier === '@angular/forms/signals') {
  return true;
}

// Slightly slower, but more accurate path.
return (
  ts.isClassDeclaration(meta.ref.node) &&
  meta.ref.node.members.some(
    (member) =>
      ts.isPropertyDeclaration(member) &&
      ts.isComputedPropertyName(member.name) &&
      ts.isIdentifier(member.name.expression) &&
      member.name.expression.text === 'ɵCONTROL',
  )
);
```

**What changed:** The `missingStructuralDirective` extended diagnostic in `@angular/compiler-cli` (check `MissingStructuralDirectiveCheck` in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/extended/checks/missing_structural_directive/index.ts`) previously bailed out entirely when the element carrying an un-imported structural directive had *any* matched directives (`if (symbol?.directives.length) return [];`), producing false negatives — no `MISSING_STRUCTURAL_DIRECTIVE` warning was reported (angular/angular#64467). Now the check only bails out when one of the matched directives' selectors actually matches the desugared attribute form of the structural directive (`dir.selector?.includes('[<name>]')`), and `ngIfThen` and `ngIfElse` were added to `KNOWN_CONTROL_FLOW_DIRECTIVES` so `*ngIf="foo; then bar; else bam"` is not flagged (commit 1f389b8b97, PR #64579). https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64579, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1f389b8b97600ee382ff842e066abc2ca31c442f.patch, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/64467

**Affects:** Applications using extended diagnostics where a template element uses a structural directive that is **not** imported into the component *and* also has another (unrelated) directive applied — these previously compiled cleanly and now emit the `MISSING_STRUCTURAL_DIRECTIVE` warning during `ng build`/`ng serve` type-checking. Also templates using `*ngIf` with `then`/`else`, which are explicitly not flagged.

**Action:** required: re-verify the build after upgrading; if the newly reported `MISSING_STRUCTURAL_DIRECTIVE` warnings appear, add the missing structural directive (e.g. a custom `*foo` directive, or `NgIf`/`NgFor`/`NgSwitch`) to the component's `imports`. No code or configuration change is otherwise needed — this is a diagnostic-correctness fix, not an API change.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// index.ts — before (any matched directive suppressed the diagnostic)
const symbol = ctx.templateTypeChecker.getSymbolOfNode(node, component);
if (symbol?.directives.length) {
  return [];
}

// after (only a directive matching [foo] suppresses it)
const hasStructuralDirective = symbol?.directives.some((dir) =>
  dir.selector?.includes(`[${customStructuralDirective.name}]`),
);
if (hasStructuralDirective) return [];
```
```html
<!-- now reported as missingStructuralDirective if BarDirective is imported but the
     *foo directive is not -->
<div *foo="bar" [bar]></div>
```

**What changed:** The compiler-cli internal options `_enableBlockSyntax` and `_enableLetSyntax` were removed from `InternalOptions` (packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/core/api/src/options.ts). Instead of honoring these flags, `NgCompiler` now derives the settings from the installed `@angular/core` version: `enableBlockSyntax = this.angularCoreVersion === null || coreVersionSupportsFeature(this.angularCoreVersion, '>= 17.0.0')` and `enableLetSyntax = ... '>= 18.1.0'` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/7fd3db04232d63f1b48ec389bbb62d9ca277fcf9.patch, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63787). The language service also stopped translating the `enableBlockSyntax: false` / `enableLetSyntax: false` project config options into those internal compiler flags in `parseNgCompilerOptions` (language_service.ts). Previously `_enableBlockSyntax` defaulted to true and was used by the language service to disable block syntax for pre-v17 projects; the version check now covers that case automatically.

**Affects:** Applications or tooling that passed `_enableBlockSyntax` or `_enableLetSyntax` in Angular compiler-cli `InternalOptions`, or set `enableBlockSyntax: false` / `enableLetSyntax: false` in the Angular language service project configuration, to opt out of block (`@if`/`@for`) or `@let` template syntax.

**Action:** none — for normal application upgrades nothing must change; block syntax is enabled when `@angular/core` is >= 17.0.0 and `@let` when >= 18.1.0. Custom integrators embedding compiler-cli/language-service that relied on the removed flags must remove those options (they are now ignored/no longer exist) and re-verify that block and `@let` syntax are treated as enabled.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before (removed)
_enableBlockSyntax?: boolean;
_enableLetSyntax?: boolean;
this.enableBlockSyntax = options['_enableBlockSyntax'] ?? true;
this.enableLetSyntax = options['_enableLetSyntax'] ?? true;

// after
this.enableBlockSyntax =
  this.angularCoreVersion === null ||
  coreVersionSupportsFeature(this.angularCoreVersion, '>= 17.0.0');
this.enableLetSyntax =
  this.angularCoreVersion === null ||
  coreVersionSupportsFeature(this.angularCoreVersion, '>= 18.1.0');
```

**What changed:** The Angular compiler-cli template type checker now verifies that a component used with the signal-forms `Field` directive conforms to `FormValueControl<any>` instead of `FormValueControl<unknown>` (checkbox controls still check against `FormCheckboxControl`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/01290ab275599ee6887f9c2139a16f833eaa7071). The generated type-check block statement changed from `var _t2 = null! as i2.FormValueControl<unknown>;` to `var _t2 = null! as i2.FormValueControl<any>;` (same URL, diff in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/src/type_check_block.ts`). This fixes a regression from the new signal forms template type checking added in #64823, where custom controls implementing `FormValueControl<string>` (e.g. a control with `value = model('')`) failed with `TS2322: Type 'Input' is not assignable to type 'FormValueControl<unknown>'. The types of 'value[SIGNAL].transformFn' are incompatible between these types` (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/64946). Note: error messages for genuinely non-conforming controls now read `Type 'X' is not assignable to type 'FormValueControl<any>'` with the nested detail `The types of 'required[SIGNAL].transformFn' are incompatible between these types.` instead of the previous `Types of property 'required' are incompatible.` (commit diff).

**Affects:** Applications using signal forms (`@angular/forms/signals`: `form`, `Field`, `FormValueControl`) with a custom control component implementing `FormValueControl<T>` bound via `<my-control [field]="field" />`, which under Angular 21.0.0-before-this-fix produced a spurious TS2322 under `strictTemplates`.

**Action:** none — this is a compiler-side fix; upgrading removes the false TS2322 error. Applications that worked around the error (e.g. casting field values to `any`, which the issue reports did not work anyway) can revert the workaround; re-verify that custom controls still type-check against `[field]` bindings.

**Before/after:**
```html
<!-- issue #64946: previously errored with strictTemplates -->
@Component({
  selector: 'app-root',
  imports: [Field, StringControl],
  template: '<string-control [field]="field" />',
})
class App {
  field = form(signal(''));
}

// generated type-check code before: var _t2 = null! as i2.FormValueControl<unknown>;
// generated type-check code after:  var _t2 = null! as i2.FormValueControl<any>;
```

