**What changed:** Angular 21 makes zoneless change detection the default, so an ng-update schematic `bootstrap-options-migration` (version `21.0.0`, "Migrates deprecated bootstrap options to providers", factory `./bundles/bootstrap-options-migration.cjs#migrate`) was added to `@angular/core/schematics/migrations.json` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/809a4ed8c110ca930cb1c6bad309f8bfcaf7ceb8). It preserves the previous Zone behaviour by moving zone configuration into providers: it inserts `provideZoneChangeDetection()` (imported from `@angular/core`) into the `providers` of `bootstrapApplication(...)`, `createApplication(...)`, the root `@NgModule` of `platformBrowser().bootstrapModule(...)` / `platformBrowserDynamic().bootstrapModule(...)`, and `TestBed.initTestEnvironment(...)` calls; it skips a call when `provideZoneChangeDetection()` or `provideZonelessChangeDetection()` is already present (inline, in a separate options const, or in `appConfig`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63042, migration.ts/migration.spec.ts in the commit diff). For `bootstrapModule` it removes the second options argument, translating `ngZoneEventCoalescing`/`ngZoneRunCoalescing` into `provideZoneChangeDetection({ eventCoalescing: true, runCoalescing: true })`, an `ngZone: <CustomZoneClass>` into `{provide: NgZone, useClass: <CustomZoneClass>}`, and `ngZone: 'noop'` into a generated `NoopNgZone` class plus `@NgModule({providers: [{provide: NgZone, useClass: NoopNgZone}]})` module added to `imports`. Non-migratable `ngZone` values are re-added with the TODO comment `// TODO: BootstrapOptions are deprecated & ignored. Configure NgZone in the providers array of the application module instead.`

**Affects:** applications/units that call `bootstrapApplication`, `createApplication`, `platformBrowser().bootstrapModule` / `platformBrowserDynamic().bootstrapModule`, or `TestBed.initTestEnvironment` and do not already declare `provideZoneChangeDetection()` or `provideZonelessChangeDetection()`.

**Action:** required: run `ng update` so the `bootstrap-options-migration` schematic applies, then review its output — especially `ngZone`, `ngZoneEventCoalescing`, `ngZoneRunCoalescing` conversions and any inserted TODO comments — and decide deliberately whether Zone change detection should really stay enabled or be replaced with `provideZonelessChangeDetection()`. Note the schematic only edits the file where bootstrap is called (e.g. `main.ts`, `main.server.ts`), not `app.config.ts` / `app.config.server.ts`, spreading the existing config as `{...appConfig, providers: [provideZoneChangeDetection(), ...appConfig.providers]}`.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before (main.ts)
import { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';
bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, appConfig);

// after (main.ts)
import { provideZoneChangeDetection } from "@angular/core";
import { bootstrapApplication } from '@angular/platform-browser';
bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {...appConfig, providers: [provideZoneChangeDetection(), ...appConfig.providers]});
```

**What changed:** Angular 21.0 adds an `applicationProviders` property to the options object accepted by `PlatformRef.bootstrapModule` and (the deprecated) `PlatformRef.bootstrapModuleFactory`. In `bootstrapModule`, `compilerOptions` is now typed as `(CompilerOptions & BootstrapOptions & {applicationProviders?: Array<Provider | EnvironmentProviders>})` or an array of that type; `bootstrapModuleFactory` accepts `options?: BootstrapOptions & {applicationProviders?: Array<Provider | EnvironmentProviders>}` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64354, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/2a7a5de53fd6fb5714c06c63dd1dad5718086083). These providers are merged into `allAppProviders` at bootstrap alongside `provideZonelessChangeDetectionInternal()` and `errorHandlerEnvironmentInitializer` (commit `2a7a5de53f`). The commit also removed the internal helpers `setZoneProvidersForNextBootstrap`/`getAndClearAdditionalProviders` and the private export `ɵsetZoneProvidersForNextBootstrap`, refactoring `downgradeModule` in `@angular/upgrade/static` to pass `internalProvideZoneChangeDetection({})` directly via `applicationProviders`.

**Affects:** Applications using `PlatformRef.bootstrapModule` / `bootstrapModuleFactory` (NgModule bootstrap) or `downgradeModule` from `@angular/upgrade/static`. New optional parameter; existing call sites compile unchanged.

**Action:** none (this is a new, additive capability; no existing call requires modification).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// After (v21.0): new optional applicationProviders in the options object
platformBrowserDynamic(extraProviders).bootstrapModule(Ng2Module, {
  applicationProviders: [provideZoneChangeDetection()],
});

// v21.0 API signature (goldens/public-api/core/index.api.md)
export class PlatformRef {
  bootstrapModule<M>(moduleType: Type<M>, compilerOptions?: (CompilerOptions & BootstrapOptions & {
    applicationProviders?: Array<Provider | EnvironmentProviders>;
  }) | Array<CompilerOptions & BootstrapOptions & {
    applicationProviders?: Array<Provider | EnvironmentProviders>;
  }>): Promise<NgModuleRef<M>>;
  bootstrapModuleFactory<M>(moduleFactory: NgModuleFactory<M>, options?: BootstrapOptions & {
    applicationProviders?: Array<Provider | EnvironmentProviders>;
  }): Promise<NgModuleRef<M>>;
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 21 removed the module-level global platform injector used during server rendering. `bootstrapApplication` in a server entry now receives a request-scoped `BootstrapContext` (third argument) providing an isolated platform injector per render, preventing state leakage between concurrent SSR requests. A schematic is provided to automatically update `main.server.ts` files to pass the `BootstrapContext` to the `bootstrapApplication` call. Additionally, `getPlatform()` now returns `null` and `destroyPlatform()` is a no-op when running in a server environment. Listed under core Breaking Changes in the v21.0.0 release notes. (`@angular/core`) (#63562 — https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63562, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/28926ba92cf3da7e45a7b8938bba49febdf58eb7, https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/21.0.0)

**Affects:** applications with server-side rendering entry points (`main.server.ts`) that call `bootstrapApplication`, and code that calls `getPlatform()` / `destroyPlatform()` in a server environment.

**Action:** required: let the provided schematic update `main.server.ts` so the bootstrap function accepts a `BootstrapContext` and forwards it as the third argument to `bootstrapApplication`; verify no server code depends on the return value of `getPlatform()` (now `null`) or on side effects of `destroyPlatform()` (now a no-op) on the server.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before
const bootstrap = () => bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, config);

// After
const bootstrap = (context: BootstrapContext) =>
  bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, config, context);
```

**What changed:** The `SimpleChanges` interface in `@angular/core` now accepts an optional generic type argument. When a component class is supplied (e.g. `SimpleChanges<MyComponent>`), accessing input keys in `ngOnChanges(changes: SimpleChanges)` becomes type-checked, producing a compilation error on typos or unknown property names. Without a generic argument, `SimpleChanges` falls back to the previous untyped behaviour for backwards compatibility. All keys are optional because any given key may be absent in a specific `ngOnChanges` invocation, and the values of input signals (`InputSignal`, `ModelSignal`) are unwrapped to match the runtime behaviour. This addresses #17560. (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64535) (https://v21.angular.dev/api/core/SimpleChanges)
**Affects:** Components and directives that implement `ngOnChanges` and read from the `changes` parameter.
**Action:** required: re-verify the `ngOnChanges` logic; optionally add the component type as the generic argument (e.g. `SimpleChanges<YourComponent>`) to enable stricter typing and address any resulting "possibly undefined" errors, since the generic argument is optional and existing code compiles unchanged.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// API doc: Pass the current class or this as the first generic argument
// for stronger type checking (e.g. SimpleChanges<YourComponent>).
ngOnChanges(changes: SimpleChanges<YourComponent>) { ... }
```

**What changed:** Angular 21.0.0 adds support for customizing `IntersectionObserver` options for the `on viewport`, `prefetch on viewport` and `hydrate on viewport` `@defer` triggers. The options must be a static object literal passed in the trigger expression, e.g. `@defer (on viewport({trigger, rootMargin: '123px', threshold: 0.5}))` (the commit message shows the shorthand `@defer (on viewport(trigger, {rootMargin: '123px'})`, while the merged PR documents the combined-object form) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64130, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ad2376435b4bcfdb695d841272f8234ab2a7cca5). Internally, the runtime instructions `ɵɵdeferOnViewport`, `ɵɵdeferPrefetchOnViewport` and `ɵɵdeferHydrateOnViewport` gained an extra `options?: IntersectionObserverInit` parameter; `createIntersectionObserver()` in `@angular/core/primitives/defer` now accepts `IntersectionObserverInit`, and instead of one global `IntersectionObserver`, observers are cached per distinct options key (derived from `rootMargin` and `threshold`), so identical options share an observer and different options create separate observers (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ad2376435b4bcfdb695d841272f8234ab2a7cca5). Hydration serialization gained `HydrateViewportTriggerDetails` with `intersectionObserverOptions`, and `BlockSummary.hydrate.viewport` changed from `boolean` to `true | IntersectionObserverInit | null` (internal `@internal`/codeGen APIs).

**Affects:** No existing application behavior changes — this is purely additive. It applies to applications that want offset/root-margin/threshold control for viewport-based `@defer` loading or hydration. Existing `on viewport` / `prefetch on viewport` / `hydrate on viewport` triggers without options behave as before.

**Action:** none (optional: to use the feature, add a static object literal with `IntersectionObserverInit` options such as `rootMargin` and `threshold` to the viewport trigger in the `@defer` template expression; options must be static, not a variable or expression).

**Before/after:**
```html
<!-- Before: no way to customize the IntersectionObserver -->
@defer (on viewport(trigger)) {Hello}

<!-- After (Angular 21): static object literal with IntersectionObserver options -->
@defer (on viewport({trigger, rootMargin: '123px', threshold: 0.5})) {Hello}
```

**What changed:** Angular 21.0.0 introduces inline regular expression literal syntax in Angular template expressions (interpolations and bindings). Before 21.0.0, expressions used inside Angular templates such as `[title]="/foo/.test(bar)"` were not supported. As of 21.0.0, this syntax is parsed as a `RegularExpressionLiteral` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63887) and represented in the template AST as `RegularExpressionLiteral` with the `body` and `flags` properties (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/539717f58a9bff1f8aacc857657b7df573d0bb70/packages/compiler/src/expression_parser/ast.ts).
**Affects:** All existing applications using Angular are unaffected because there is no new syntax to migrate to. However, Angular library authors or applications that define custom parsers for Angular templates, or implement the `AstVisitor` interface from `@angular/compiler` to traverse the template AST, are affected because the `AstVisitor` interface now requires the implementation of the `visitRegularExpressionLiteral` method.
**Action:** required: Implement the `visitRegularExpressionLiteral` method in any class that implements the `AstVisitor` interface to prevent compilation errors.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before: `visitRegularExpressionLiteral` does not exist
export interface AstVisitor {
  // ...
}

// After: `visitRegularExpressionLiteral` is required
export interface AstVisitor {
  // ...
  visitRegularExpressionLiteral(ast: RegularExpressionLiteral, context: any): any;
  // ...
}
```

**What changed:** In Angular 21.0.0-next.6, signal-forms control binding (`[control]` / `Field`) started throwing `RuntimeError: NG0318: '<input>' is an invalid control host` for genuine native `<input>`, `<select>`, `<textarea>` elements whenever a directive applied to that element injects `ViewContainerRef` (the runtime then inserts a container comment node that threw off the DOM-node-based native-control detection) (#64362, #64368). The fix (commit `ab415f3d7f`) changes `packages/core/src/render3/instructions/control.ts` so `isNativeControl`, `isNumericInput` and `isTextControl` now inspect the `TNode` (`TNodeType.Element`, tag name `input`/`textarea`/`select`, and the static `type` attribute for numeric types: `date`, `datetime-local`, `month`, `number`, `range`, `time`, `week`) instead of the rendered DOM element (`HTMLInputElement`/`HTMLSelectElement`/`HTMLTextAreaElement` instanceof checks). The commit message notes this also makes the logic "a bit less prone to breaking during SSR."

**Affects:** Applications on 21.0.0-next.6 using signal forms (`Field` / `[control]` / `[field]`) bound to a native form control element that also matches a directive injecting `ViewContainerRef` (e.g. a `@Directive({selector: 'input'})` doing `inject(ViewContainerRef)`). This was a regression introduced and fixed within the 21.0.0 line; apps upgrading 20.3 → 21.0.0 final are unaffected.

**Action:** none — no application code change is required; the fix is internal to `@angular/core`. Applications pinned to 21.0.0-next.6 should move to a build containing this commit (21.0.0 final or later).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before (21.0.0-next.6): checked the rendered DOM node
function isNativeControl(element: unknown): element is NativeControlElement {
  return (
    element instanceof HTMLInputElement ||
    element instanceof HTMLSelectElement ||
    element instanceof HTMLTextAreaElement
  );
}

// after (fix): checks the TNode instead
function isNativeControl(tNode: TNode): tNode is TElementNode {
  if (tNode.type !== TNodeType.Element) {
    return false;
  }
  const tagName = tNode.value;
  return tagName === 'input' || tagName === 'textarea' || tagName === 'select';
}
```
(https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ab415f3d7f23cef8e00595e9cf6af2c8b764a8ae.patch)

**What changed:** Angular 21.0.0 removes the special-case in `ChangeDetectionSchedulerImpl` (`packages/core/src/change_detection/scheduling/zoneless_scheduling_impl.ts`) that disabled the internal change detection scheduler when zoneless was not explicitly enabled (`ZONELESS_ENABLED`) but `Zone` was undefined or a `NoopNgZone` was in use. The `disableScheduling` field and the `force` flag path in `shouldScheduleTick` were deleted, so the scheduler "is now enabled in _all_ applications" (#63846, commit 1352fbdbf2). Per the 21.0.0 release notes: "Using a combination of `provideZoneChangeDetection` while also removing ZoneJS polyfills will no longer result in the internal scheduler being disabled. All Angular applications now consistenly use the same scheduler, and those with the Zone change detection provider include additional automatic scheduling behaviors based on `NgZone` stabilization." The same release notes also state that `ignoreChangesOutsideZone` "is no longer available as an option for configuring ZoneJS change detection behavior," and that Angular no longer provides a change detection scheduler for ZoneJS-based change detection by default — `provideZoneChangeDetection` must be added to `bootstrapApplication` providers or `AppModule` (covered by an automated migration) (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/21.0.0).

(https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1352fbdbf2542c39715045c7a6c0f6aa41516b02, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63846)

**Affects:** Applications that used the old-style "zoneless" pattern: keeping `provideZoneChangeDetection` in providers while removing the ZoneJS polyfill (e.g. from `polyfills`), or otherwise running with `Zone` undefined / `NoopNgZone` without enabling zoneless — these previously got no automatic scheduler and relied on manual change detection. Also affected: apps passing the removed `ignoreChangesOutsideZone` option to `provideZoneChangeDetection`.

**Action:** required: re-verify change detection timing in apps that ran without Zone while keeping `provideZoneChangeDetection` — the internal scheduler now runs and change detection may trigger automatically where it previously did not. Remove `ignoreChangesOutsideZone` from any `provideZoneChangeDetection` call. Otherwise migrate to explicit zoneless (`provideExperimentalZonelessChangeDetection` in 20.x terminology) or restore the ZoneJS polyfill and add `provideZoneChangeDetection` to `bootstrapApplication`/`AppModule` providers as intended; run the automated migration for the provider addition.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Removed in 21.0.0 (zoneless_scheduling_impl.ts) — old "no Zone" special case:
this.disableScheduling =
  !this.zonelessEnabled &&
  // NoopNgZone without enabling zoneless means no scheduling whatsoever
  (this.ngZone instanceof NoopNgZone ||
    // The same goes for the lack of Zone without enabling zoneless scheduling
    !this.zoneIsDefined);

// 21.0.0: scheduler always enabled
private shouldScheduleTick(): boolean {
  if (this.appRef.destroyed) {
    return false;
  }
```

**What changed:** Angular 21 raised its minimum supported TypeScript version from 5.8 to 5.9. In `@angular/compiler-cli`, the `peerDependencies` range for `typescript` changed to `">=5.9 <6.0"` and the runtime guard in `typescript_support.ts` now uses `MIN_TS_VERSION = '5.9.0'` and `MAX_TS_VERSION = '6.0.0'`; `verifySupportedTypeScriptVersion()` throws `The Angular Compiler requires TypeScript >=5.9 and <6.0 but <version> was found instead.` for out-of-range compilers. The `integration/typings_test_ts58` test project (pinned `typescript: 5.8.2`) was deleted (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/c0791e1887590b862bfed9333c5c90be3ac487d0, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63589).

**Affects:** Applications that pin or install `typescript` 5.8.x (or anything below 5.9) in `package.json`; projects on TypeScript >= 5.9 and < 6.0 are unaffected.

**Action:** required: bump the `typescript` devDependency to a version satisfying `>=5.9 <6.0`, then re-run `ng build` / `ngc` to verify compilation, since Angular's compiler rejects older TypeScript at startup.

**Before/after:**
```jsonc
// package.json (devDependencies) — before: "typescript": "~5.8.x" (rejected by Angular 21)
{
  "devDependencies": {
    "typescript": ">=5.9 <6.0"
  }
}
```
```jsonc
// packages/compiler-cli/package.json peerDependencies (v21)
"peerDependencies": { "@angular/compiler": "0.0.0-PLACEHOLDER", "typescript": ">=5.9 <6.0" }
```

**What changed:** In Angular 21.0.0, `@for` (`ɵɵrepeater` in `packages/core/src/render3/instructions/control_flow.ts`) now passes the previous reactive consumer (`prevConsumer`) into the internal `reconcile(...)` function (`packages/core/src/render3/list_reconciliation.ts`), which gained a new fourth parameter `reactiveConsumer: ReactiveNode | null`. `reconcile` now wraps the read of `newCollection.length` (array fast path) and `newCollection[Symbol.iterator]()` (iterable slow path) with `setActiveConsumer(reactiveConsumer)` / `setActiveConsumer(null)` (from `@angular/core/primitives/signals`), so reactive reads triggered by proxy objects — such as signal forms' `Field` — during those accesses are properly tracked before the active consumer is cleared. Previously these reads happened after the consumer was cleared, so they were not recorded as dependencies and the view did not re-render when the proxy's underlying signals changed (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64113, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/aa389a691bc2e5726a0ded73d30962c29faab680).

**Affects:** Applications using the built-in `@for` control flow with a collection that is a `Proxy` (e.g. signal forms' `Field`) whose `length` or `Symbol.iterator` getters read signals; in 20.3 such views could silently fail to update. Not an API break for app code — `reconcile` is internal (the only signature change visible in the diff is in `packages/core/src/render3/list_reconciliation.ts` and its specs).

**Action:** none — this is an internal bug fix; applications relying on `@for` over signal-form `Field` / other signal-reading proxies should simply re-verify that those views now re-render when the underlying signals change.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/core/src/render3/list_reconciliation.ts (21.0.0)
export function reconcile<T, V>(
  liveCollection: LiveCollection<T, V>,
  newCollection: Iterable<V> | undefined | null,
  trackByFn: TrackByFunction<V>,
  reactiveConsumer: ReactiveNode | null, // new 4th arg (20.x had 3 args)
): void {
  ...
  if (Array.isArray(newCollection)) {
    setActiveConsumer(reactiveConsumer);
    let newEndIdx = newCollection.length - 1;
    setActiveConsumer(null);
```

**What changed:** Angular 21 adds validation for the `APP_ID` injection token (`@angular/core`). At application bootstrap (both `bootstrapApplication`/`internalCreateApplication` and NgModule `PlatformRef.bootstrap`, dev mode only via `ngDevMode`), a new `ENVIRONMENT_INITIALIZER` provider (`validAppIdInitializer`) checks the injected `APP_ID` against `/^[a-zA-Z0-9\-_]+$/`. A non-matching value now throws `RuntimeError` with new code `RuntimeErrorCode.INVALID_APP_ID = 211` (`NG0211`): `APP_ID value "..." is not alphanumeric. The APP_ID must be a string of alphanumeric characters. (a-zA-Z0-9), hyphens (-) and underscores (_) are allowed.` Previously invalid IDs (e.g. containing `:`) were silently accepted and could generate broken CSS selectors, since `:` is a pseudo-selector separator (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63252, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/fec7c288e96dd32f5861124384dbef4d5350d437). PR was labeled `breaking changes`, `target: major`; fixes #63251. The default `APP_ID` (`'ng'` plus generated random ids) is unaffected.

**Affects:** Applications that provide a custom `APP_ID` (e.g. `{provide: APP_ID, useValue: '...'} `) whose value contains characters other than `a-zA-Z0-9`, `-`, `_`, and use `bootstrapApplication` or `platformBrowserDynamic().bootstrapModule`. Validation runs in dev mode only.

**Action:** required: change any custom `APP_ID` value to use only alphanumeric characters, hyphens and underscores; verify the app bootstraps without `NG0211`.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before: bootstrap succeeded, but could produce broken CSS selectors
{provide: APP_ID, useValue: 'foo:bar'} // throws NG0211 in v21 dev mode

// After: only alphanumerics, '-' and '_' are allowed
{provide: APP_ID, useValue: 'foo-bar'}
```

**What changed:** TestBed has an internal error handler (`INTERNAL_APPLICATION_ERROR_HANDLER`) that rethrows errors so they are not silently swallowed in tests. Previously, `internalProvideZoneChangeDetection` (used by `provideZoneChangeDetection`) supplied its own `INTERNAL_APPLICATION_ERROR_HANDLER` provider, so tests that added `provideZoneChangeDetection` to TestBed providers overrode TestBed's error handler and errors were not rethrown. In this commit the `INTERNAL_APPLICATION_ERROR_HANDLER` provider was removed from `internalProvideZoneChangeDetection` (packages/core/src/change_detection/scheduling/ng_zone_scheduling.ts); the `zone.runOutsideAngular(...)` wrapping was moved into the default handler in `packages/core/src/error_handler.ts`. As a result, "Errors in the test will now be rethrown, regardless of the usage of `provideZoneChangeDetection`. Tests should be adjusted to prevent or account for these errors. As in previous major versions, this behavior can be disabled with `rethrowApplicationErrors: false` in `configureTestingModule` as a last resort." (test only) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/d399d7d02b66c485cc5479dabd349d017a002692, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63404)

**Affects:** Test files that pass `provideZoneChangeDetection` (or other zone change-detection providers) in TestBed/`TestBed.configureTestingModule` providers and contain application errors that were previously silently swallowed.

**Action:** required: re-verify affected specs; fix or explicitly account for errors that are now rethrown and fail tests. If a spec must retain the old silent behaviour, set `rethrowApplicationErrors: false` in `configureTestingModule` as a last resort. No production code changes.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (ng_zone_scheduling.ts): provideZoneChangeDetection overrode TestBed's handler
{
  provide: INTERNAL_APPLICATION_ERROR_HANDLER,
  useFactory: () => { /* handler that bypassed TestBed rethrow */ },
},

// After: no such provider here; only
{
  provide: SCHEDULE_IN_ROOT_ZONE,
  useValue: scheduleInRootZone ?? SCHEDULE_IN_ROOT_ZONE_DEFAULT,
},
```

**What changed:** The `ignoreChangesOutsideZone` option, previously available to configure ZoneJS change detection scheduling behavior, has been removed from `NgZoneOptions` and related configuration interfaces (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/92e09adc0a191ec599915e20b0835bf455bc572b). This option was originally introduced in Angular v18 to allow developers to opt out of the new behavior that scheduled change detection for events occurring outside the `NgZone`, but it was deprecated in v18.2. The Angular team determined that the v18 feature works as intended and that disabling it via this option is not necessary (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62700).

**Affects:** Applications explicitly setting `ignoreChangesOutsideZone` to `true` in their `NgZone` configuration or `provideZoneChangeDetection` providers.

**Action:** required: Remove any usage of `ignoreChangesOutsideZone` from application configuration. If you need to prevent change detection for tasks that run outside the Angular zone, ensure they are wrapped in `NgZone.runOutsideAngular`.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (v18)
provideZoneChangeDetection({ ignoreChangesOutsideZone: true });

// After (v21)
// Option removed; default behavior applies.
provideZoneChangeDetection();
```

**What changed:** Angular v21 no longer provides the `ZoneJS`-based change detection scheduler internally by default, making applications Zoneless by default and allowing tree-shaking of the Zone change detection providers. If `provideZoneChangeDetection` is used while ZoneJS polyfills are removed, the internal scheduler is no longer disabled, and the application now uses the standard scheduler with additional automatic scheduling based on `NgZone` stabilization. Additionally, the `ignoreChangesOutsideZone` configuration option for ZoneJS change detection has been removed. (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/45fed3d2011bf6feffa8ee1365a5c88d603f826c, https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/21.0.0)
**Affects:** Applications relying on ZoneJS change detection (by default or via `provideZoneChangeDetection`), especially those using the `ignoreChangesOutsideZone` option or attempting to disable the scheduler by removing polyfills while keeping the provider.
**Action:** required: Add `provideZoneChangeDetection` to the providers array of your `bootstrapApplication` function or your `AppModule`. Re-verify change detection behavior and ensure `ignoreChangesOutsideZone` is removed from any `provideZoneChangeDetection` configuration.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (v20): ZoneJS change detection was provided by default
bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [
    provideZoneChangeDetection({ ignoreChangesOutsideZone: true })
  ]
});

// After (v21): Must explicitly add provideZoneChangeDetection
bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [
    provideZoneChangeDetection() // ignoreChangesOutsideZone is removed
  ]
});
```

**What changed:** `moduleId` was removed from `Component` metadata as a breaking change in Angular 21.0.0 (commit a5e5dbbc16, PR #63482). The property had been deprecated in #49496 because "it had no effect" (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a5e5dbbc16f605cce6dd72a82ddb9110e655a89b, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63482). The v21 `Component` API page no longer lists `moduleId` among the decorator's options (previously used as `moduleId: module.id` to resolve relative `templateUrl`/`styleUrls` paths) (https://v21.angular.dev/api/core/Component).

**Affects:** Applications that set `moduleId` in the `@Component()` decorator metadata (e.g. `moduleId: module.id`). Since the property had no effect before removal, deleting it does not change runtime behavior; it will now be a compile error/excess property in strict TypeScript.

**Action:** required: remove any `moduleId` entries (typically `moduleId: module.id`) from all `@Component()` decorator metadata. No replacement option exists; relative `templateUrl`/`styleUrls` resolution is unaffected since the property was already inert.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (Angular 20.x, deprecated no-op)
@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  moduleId: module.id,
  templateUrl: './example.html',
})

// After (Angular 21)
@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  templateUrl: './example.html',
})
```
*(Shape of the removed property inferred from the deprecation of the `moduleId` `@Component` property in PR #49496; fetched pages confirm removal but do not include a literal snippet.)*

**What changed:** The `interpolation` option on `@Component` (used to change the delimiters from `{{ }}`) has been removed. Only the default `{{ ... }}` delimiters are supported. This option was previously deprecated in Angular 20 (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/55778) with the message `DEPRECATION: @Component.interpolation is deprecated. Use Angular's delimiters instead.` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/55778). A migration schematic (referenced as PR #634820) is noted as coming in a follow-up PR (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63474).
**Affects:** Components that set the `interpolation` property in their `@Component` decorator.
**Action:** required: remove the `interpolation` property from all `@Component` decorators and rely on the default `{{ ... }}` delimiters. If a custom delimiter was in use to avoid conflicts (e.g., with SSR), the component templates must be reviewed.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (deprecated/invalid in v21)
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  template: `[[value]]`, // Using custom delimiters like [[ ]]
  interpolation: ['[[', ']]']
})
export class ExampleComponent {}

// After (Required)
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  template: `{{value}}` // Must use default {{ }}
})
export class ExampleComponent {}
```

**What changed:** For components that use signal inputs (created via `input()` / `model()` / `input.required()`) and are exposed as a custom element through `@angular/elements`, the custom-element property getter for a signal input now returns the current value instead of the `Signal` itself. Previously `elementRef.newInput` returned a callable signal requiring `elementRef.newInput()` to read the value; decorator (`@Input()`) properties were read directly (`elementRef.oldInput`). After the fix, both decorator and signal inputs are read directly (`elementRef.newInput`) and set the same way (`elementRef.newInput = 'new value'`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/be0455adda7d92f741105b3599e7922f099cc024) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/62113) (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/62097)
**Affects:** Applications that convert a component to a custom element using `@angular/elements` and read signal-input values from the element instance via `elementRef.<signalInput>()`; setter usage and `@Input()` reads are unchanged.
**Action:** required: in code that reads signal-input values from the custom-element instance, remove the call parentheses so the property returns the value directly; verify TypeScript typing for these reads, since they previously typed as a `Signal`.
**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before
@Input() oldInput = '';
newInput = input('');

elementRef.oldInput;      // value
elementRef.newInput();    // value (getter returned the Signal)

// After
elementRef.oldInput;      // value
elementRef.newInput;      // value (getter now returns the value directly)
```

**What changed:** Angular 21.0.0 adds a new `debounce()` rule to signal forms: `debounce(path, durationOrDebouncer)` where `durationOrDebouncer` is either a number (duration in milliseconds) or a `Debouncer` function (the docs additionally accept the literal `'blur'` to debounce until blur) (<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/a278ee358c4d111cf29eb8d3d8eb1fe1799c8495/packages/forms/signals/src/api/debounce.ts>, <https://angular.dev/api/forms/signals/debounce>). Applied in a schema via `apply(path, debounce(path, 500))`, it delays UI-to-model synchronization until the field is touched or the debounced update resolves. This commit also changes `FieldState`: `controlValue` is a new signal property representing the value in the UI control, while `value` (the model value) may now lag behind `controlValue` when a `debounce()` rule is active. The intent is that expensive/slow validation reacts to the debounced `value` rather than the faster-changing `controlValue`; directly writing `value` immediately updates `controlValue` and cancels pending debounced updates. When multiple `debounce()` rules apply to one field, the last currently active rule wins (`DEBOUNCER` aggregate metadata key reduces by picking the last item); rules propagate to child fields unless they define their own (<https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a278ee358c4d111cf29eb8d3d8eb1fe1799c8495>, <https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/a278ee358c4d111cf29eb8d3d8eb1fe1799c8495/packages/forms/signals/src/field/debounce.ts>). The `debounce` function is marked `@experimental 21.0.0`.

**Affects:** Applications using signal forms (`@angular/forms/signals`) whose validators or logic read `FieldState.value()`. Behavior of `value()` is unchanged when no `debounce()` rule is applied; code reading `value()` from code paths that previously reflected UI input immediately may now observe a lagging model value only if a `debounce()` rule is introduced.

**Action:** none — upgrading applications that do not call the new `debounce()` rule need no changes; re-verify any signal-form validators that depend on immediate `value()` updates after UI input.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
import {form, schema, apply, debounce} from '@angular/forms/signals';

this.userForm = form(user, schema((userPath) => {
  // New: delay model updates by 500ms; validators see debounced value
  apply(userPath.username, debounce(userPath.username, 500));
}));
```

**What changed:** In the signals forms API (`@angular/forms/signals`), the internal `FieldNodeState` class gained a private computed `isNonInteractive` (`hidden() || disabled() || readonly()`), and the `touched` and `dirty` signals now ignore a field's own `selfTouched`/`selfDirty` flag when the field is non-interactive (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/d201cd2c2bdb418fd1b595320855c35eb91e1e5b.patch). Behaviour: calling `f().markAsTouched()` or `f().markAsDirty()` on a field that is `hidden`, `disabled`, or `readonly` no longer makes `f().touched()` / `f().dirty()` return `true`; the PR notes `isNonInteractive` was left private, not exposed as public API (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63633). Previously these TODO cases marked such fields touched/dirty. Also, a field already marked touched/dirty that later becomes hidden/disabled/readonly reverts to untouched/pristine, and becomes touched/dirty again when it becomes interactive again (test "should become pristine when field becomes non-interactive after being marked dirty").

**Affects:** Applications using signal forms (`form()`, `FormResource`) that call `markAsTouched()`/`markAsDirty()` (directly or e.g. via `submit()`-style flows) on fields guarded with `hidden`, `disabled`, or `readonly`, or that render error/UI based on `field().touched()` / `field().dirty()` for such fields.

**Action:** none — no code, dependency, or configuration change is required; re-verify any UI logic that showed validation messages or "has been edited" indicators for hidden/disabled/readonly fields, since those fields now report `touched() === false` and `dirty() === false`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
