**What changed:** In Angular 21 the signal forms `Field` directive (`[field]`) now binds the `invalid` state to custom controls. Previously, when using `[field]` with a component implementing `FormValueControl`, the core render3 instruction `updateCustomControl` (in `packages/core/src/render3/instructions/control.ts`) wrote `value`, `errors`, `disabled`, `disabledReasons`, `max`, etc. into matching directive inputs via `maybeWriteToDirectiveInput`, but never `invalid` — so a custom control's `invalid = input(false)` signal input stayed at its default even when the field was invalid. The fix adds `maybeWriteToDirectiveInput(componentDef, component, 'invalid', state.invalid);` and adds an `invalid: Signal<boolean>` member to the internal `ɵFieldState` interface in `packages/core/src/render3/interfaces/control.ts` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/9c5e969f51448aad05a7e0ac83143b4b5ae477b4, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64526). The PR is classified as a bugfix, not a breaking change.

**Affects:** Applications using signal forms (`Field` / `[field]` from `@angular/forms/signals`) with custom control components that declare an `invalid` input signal (e.g. `invalid = input(false)`) and previously worked around the missing binding (e.g. by setting it manually or reading validity another way).

**Action:** none — for typical usage the `invalid` input simply starts receiving the field's validity. Applications that manually drove `invalid` on such a custom control should re-verify that the now framework-bound input does not conflict with their workaround.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// custom control used with <my-input [field]="f" />
class CustomInput implements FormValueControl<string> {
  value = model('');
  invalid = input(false); // 20.3: never updated by [field]; 21.0: receives state.invalid
}
```

**What changed:** Updates from interop controls (controls bridged via `registerOnChange`) previously set `control.state().value.set(value)` directly, which bypassed configured debounce rules. Now `listenToInteropControl` calls `control.state().setControlValue(value)`, so any `debounce` transform configured via `form(signal(''), (p) => debounce(p, () => promise))` is honored, making value propagation asynchronous and matching the behavior of native and custom controls (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/d89e522a1f07c4b2ac7fd2b926ae44658f9394d4 and https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/b1037ec2f0555181a666f8654f5619b9d6dba5c3.patch).

**Affects:** Signal-forms applications that use an interop `ControlValueAccessor` (custom control with `NG_VALUE_ACCESSOR` / `registerOnChange`) together with the `debounce(p, ...) transform` on the same `form(...)` field.

**Action:** required: re-verify code that previously assumed `f().value()` updates synchronously after an interop control change while a `debounce` transform is configured; those updates now only land once the debounce promise resolves.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before
interopControl.registerOnChange((value: unknown) => {
  const state = control.state();
  state.value.set(value);
  state.markAsDirty();
});

// after
interopControl.registerOnChange((value: unknown) =>
  control.state().setControlValue(value));
```

**What changed:** `FormResetEvent` (from `@angular/forms`) is now emitted by `AbstractControl.reset()` itself — in `FormControl`, `FormGroup`, and `FormArray` — instead of only being pushed by the form directives. Previously it was emitted by `NgForm.resetForm()` (`this.form._events.next(new FormResetEvent(this.form))`) and `AbstractFormDirective.resetForm()` (the reactive `formGroup`/`formArray`/`formGroupNamed` directive base class), so `AbstractControl.reset()` calls never produced it — only a native `<input type="reset">`/directive `resetForm()` path did (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/c0d88c37c983991236177a0337f5cab75054abf7, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64024, fixes https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/58894). The directive-level emission lines were deleted; the control now emits `FormResetEvent` after `updateValueAndValidity`/value-set, guarded by `if (options?.emitEvent !== false)`. Consequently one `reset()` no longer produces a duplicate `FormResetEvent` for the top-level form control: e.g. `fg.reset({fc1: 'newFoo', fc2: 'newBar'})` now yields exactly `TouchedChangeEvent`, `ValueChangeEvent`, `StatusChangeEvent`, `FormResetEvent` (source = the control being reset, e.g. `fg`), and with `{emitEvent: false}` only a `TouchedChangeEvent` remains (diff, `packages/forms/test/reactive_integration_spec.ts`).

**Affects:** Applications that subscribe to `AbstractControl.events` / `ControlEvent` and filter on `FormResetEvent` (now receive extra events for programmatic `reset()` on any `FormControl`, `FormGroup`, or `FormArray`), or that relied on `FormResetEvent` being emitted twice / only via `NgForm.resetForm()` or `AbstractFormDirective.resetForm()`.

**Action:** required: re-verify every `events` subscription that branches on `FormResetEvent` — such handlers now also fire for direct `control.reset()`, `group.reset()`, and `array.reset()` calls, and `event.source` may be a nested `FormControl`/`FormGroup`/`FormArray` rather than the top-level form; add identity checks against the expected control (as the Angular tests do with `expect(event.source).toBe(fg)`) to avoid duplicate side effects. Suppress with `reset(value, {emitEvent: false})` where no event is wanted.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (Angular 20.x): NgForm / AbstractFormDirective emitted it; reset() did not.
resetForm(value: any = undefined, options: {onlySelf?: boolean; emitEvent?: boolean} = {}): void {
  this.form.reset(value, options);
  this._submittedReactive.set(false);
  if (options?.emitEvent !== false) {
    this.form._events.next(new FormResetEvent(this.form));
  }
}

// After (Angular 21.0.0): emitted inside AbstractControl subclasses' reset()
// (FormControl, FormGroup, FormArray); the directive code above is removed.
if (options?.emitEvent !== false) {
  this._events.next(new FormResetEvent(this));
}
```

**What changed:** In Angular 20.x, the signal forms `Field` directive (`packages/forms/signals/src/api/field_directive.ts`) called `controlValueAccessor.writeValue(...)` and `controlValueAccessor.setDisabledState?.(...)` directly inside the template's reactive context. If a `ControlValueAccessor` used signals internally (e.g. `this.value.set(newValue)` in `writeValue`), this threw `NG0600` ("writing to signals in a reactive context") when the CVA was bound with `[field]` (issue #64614) (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/64614). Fixed in 21.0.0 by PR #64618 / commit `a1ac9a6415`: the state values (`this.state().value()`, `this.state().disabled()`) are still read reactively, but the CVA calls `writeValue` and `setDisabledState` are now wrapped in `untracked(() => {...})` imported from `@angular/core`, so CVAs that write to signals no longer error (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64618, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a1ac9a64154c0a9206e11343b195f287dba3425d).

**Affects:** Applications using signal forms `Field` (`@angular/forms/signals`) with a custom `ControlValueAccessor` (via `NG_VALUE_ACCESSOR`) whose `writeValue`/`setDisabledState` write to signals; in 20.x these threw `NG0600`. This is a bug fix — no API change.

**Action:** none — upgrade fixes the error; re-verify that custom CVAs bound with `[field]` update their value/disabled state correctly and that `NG0600` no longer occurs.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (20.3):
controlValueAccessor.writeValue(this.state().value());
controlValueAccessor.setDisabledState?.(this.state().disabled());

// After (21.0):
const value = this.state().value();
const disabled = this.state().disabled();
untracked(() => {
  controlValueAccessor.writeValue(value);
  controlValueAccessor.setDisabledState?.(disabled);
});
```

**What changed:** In Angular 20.x signal forms, the `Field` directive (`packages/forms/signals`) did not call `state.markAsDirty()` when a bound UI control changed the value, so `field().dirty()` stayed `false` after user edits. Two fixes landed for 21.0.0:
- Commit `3529877772` / PR #64483 added `state.markAsDirty()` in `packages/core/src/render3/instructions/control.ts` in both `listenToNativeControl` (input/change events on native `input`/`textarea`) and `listenToCustomControl` (controls implementing `FormValueControl`/`FormCheckboxControl`) — fixing #64465 and #63623 (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64483, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/3529877772f7a777d467c99e3d95b465b1b1d82c.patch)
- Commit `505bde1fed` (same PR branch, #64471) added the same `state.markAsDirty()` call inside `Field.ɵinteropControlCreate()`'s `controlValueAccessor.registerOnChange(...)` callback, so `ControlValueAccessor`-backed controls behave the same. PR #64471 also restored signal-forms/reactive-forms interop removed by commit `effccffde0c5aa8f8f8d9e67013b88d1ccc762d7`, so a signal forms field can again be bound to any element or component providing a `ControlValueAccessor` (the `Field` doc comment now reads "This should only be used for backwards compatibility with reactive forms. Prefer options (1) and (2).") (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/505bde1fede95ec907c6b028db4b3c9237899f30.patch, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64471)

**Affects:** Applications using signal forms (`form()` / `field()` / the `Field` directive from `@angular/forms/signals`) that read `field().dirty()` (or `FieldState` dirty state, e.g. for styling/validation gating) on fields bound to native inputs, `FormValueControl`/`FormCheckboxControl` custom controls, or reactive-forms components with a `ControlValueAccessor`.

**Action:** required: re-verify any logic that branches on `field().dirty()` — it is now `true` after user input, whereas in 20.x it could remain `false`. Note `markAsDirty()` is triggered by control-originated value changes (user typing), not by programmatic `state.value.set(...)`/signal writes. No code, dependency, or configuration change is mandated by the sources.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// packages/forms/signals/src/api/field_directive.ts (Field.ɵinteropControlCreate)

// 20.x (before)
controlValueAccessor.registerOnChange((value: T) => this.state().value.set(value));

// 21.0 (after)
controlValueAccessor.registerOnChange((value: T) => {
  const state = this.state();
  state.value.set(value);
  state.markAsDirty();
});
```
```typescript
// packages/core/src/render3/instructions/control.ts — listenToNativeControl

// 20.x (before)
const value = control.state().value;
value.set(getNativeControlValue(element, value));

// 21.0 (after)
const state = control.state();
state.value.set(getNativeControlValue(element, state.value));
state.markAsDirty();
```

**What changed:** In the signals forms API, `FieldState` previously declared a non-optional signal for each built-in property (`max`, `maxLength`, `min`, `minLength`, `pattern`, `required`), so the `Field` directive propagated all of them to the bound control, overriding default/template-set values (e.g. clearing `required`/`max`/`min` set directly in the template). Now these properties are `undefined` unless defined in the schema: the internal `ɵFieldState` interface declares them optional (`readonly max?: Signal<number | undefined>`, `readonly maxLength?`, `readonly min?`, `readonly minLength?`, `readonly pattern?: Signal<readonly RegExp[]>`, `readonly required?: Signal<boolean>`), and `FieldNode` returns them via a new `propertyOrUndefined` helper backed by `hasProperty`. `maybeWriteToDirectiveInput` and native-control attribute writing (`setBooleanAttribute`, `setOptionalAttribute` in `updateNativeControl`) skip any property whose signal is `undefined`; `name`, `readonly`, `touched` were also added to the custom-control input list. Thus `Field` no longer binds properties not explicitly managed by the schema (e.g. `required(p, {when: ...})` still overrides a template `required` attribute, but a plain `form(signal(''))` no longer clears it) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/fd9af2afaf6c239bbbba50f2f016ecf9b83133c4, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64446).

**Affects:** Applications using signals forms (`form()` / `Field` directive from `@angular/forms/signals`) that relied on `Field` propagating default values for `required`, `max`, `min`, `maxLength`, `minLength`, or `pattern` — e.g. a custom control component declaring its own `required`/`max` inputs with non-default defaults, or template attributes like `<input [field]="f" required>` — or custom code implementing/reading `ɵFieldState` properties.

**Action:** required: re-verify that form fields still render the expected `required`, `max`, `min`, `maxLength`, `minLength`, and `pattern` values; if a value previously came from the field's default signal rather than the schema, define it explicitly in the form schema (e.g. `required(p, ...)`, `min(p, ...)`, `max(p, ...)`) or set it on the control/inputs directly. Code that reads these properties off a `FieldState`/`ɵFieldState` must handle `undefined` (they are now optional signals).

**Before/after:**
```html
<!-- Before (20.3): Field propagated required = false by default, clearing the attribute. -->
<!-- After (21.0): 'required' should be unchanged -> true (no schema-managed required) -->
<input [field]="f" required>
```
```ts
// 21.0 ɵFieldState — these are now optional (undefined unless defined in the schema)
readonly max?: Signal<number | undefined>;
readonly maxLength?: Signal<number | undefined>;
readonly min?: Signal<number | undefined>;
readonly minLength?: Signal<number | undefined>;
readonly pattern?: Signal<readonly RegExp[]>;
readonly required?: Signal<boolean>;
```

**What changed:** In signal forms, errors produced by `validateStandardSchema` (from `@angular/forms/signals`) are `StandardSchemaValidationError` instances. Previously the human-readable message was only reachable at `error.issue.message` (of type `StandardSchemaV1.Issue`), while `error.message` (part of the `ValidationError` interface) stayed `undefined` — requiring `instanceof StandardSchemaValidationError` narrowing, which is impossible in templates (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/65247). Commit `91d8d55a80` ("fix(forms): Set error message of a schema error", cherry-picked from `b41a94bc85`) changed `standardIssueToFormTreeError` in `packages/forms/signals/src/api/validators/standard_schema.ts` to call `standardSchemaError(issue, {message: issue.message})` instead of `standardSchemaError(issue)`, so the issue's message is now copied onto the top-level `error.message` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/91d8d55a80a1d1894827ef06e38e56de6e661575, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/21.0.0/packages/forms/signals/src/api/validators/standard_schema.ts).

**Affects:** Applications using `validateStandardSchema` (e.g. Zod validators) in signal forms that read `error.message`, or that worked around the missing message via a pipe / type narrowing on `StandardSchemaValidationError`. Note: `validateStandardSchema`, `standardSchemaError` and `StandardSchemaValidationError` are marked `@experimental 21.0.0`, so they only exist in this new API surface.

**Action:** none — no code change is required; previously-`undefined` `error.message` is now populated. Re-verify any custom code that branched on `error.message === undefined` for schema errors or that unwrapped `error.issue.message` manually (e.g. a custom `ErrorMessagePipe`), since `error.message` can now be used directly like `email(path.email, { message: 'Enter a valid email' })` errors (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/65247).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before (20.3 behaviour of the equivalent code path):
return addDefaultField(standardSchemaError(issue), target);
// after (21.0.0):
return addDefaultField(standardSchemaError(issue, {message: issue.message}), target);

// test assertion added in 21.0.0:
expect(f.age().errors()[0].message).toBe('Age must be non-negative');
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/forms/signals` field bindings, the internal `ɵɵcontrol` instruction (`packages/core/src/render3/instructions/control.ts`) now does per-property change detection: previously every change detection cycle wrote all field state properties (`value`, `errors`, `invalid`, `disabled`, `disabledReasons`, `name`, `readonly`, `touched`, `max`, `maxLength`, `min`, `minLength`, `pattern`, `required`) to the custom control's directive inputs or to native element attributes; now each value is compared with `Object.is` against the previously bound value (stored in a new extra variable slot, so `vars` for `[field]` bindings grows from 2 to 3) and only written when it changed (). Additionally, `ɵFieldState.pattern` and `ɵFieldState.required` changed from optional (`readonly pattern?: Signal<readonly RegExp[]>`, `readonly required?: Signal<boolean>`) to always-present signals; `FieldNode.pattern` now returns `computed(() => [])` (`EMPTY`) and `FieldNode.required` returns `computed(() => false)` (`FALSE`) when the `PATTERN`/`REQUIRED` metadata is not set, instead of `undefined` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/507b3466eec648a706f10d2805e67e53522e9654).

**Affects:** Applications using the signals forms `field` directive (`@angular/forms/signals`) that either (a) read `FieldState.pattern` / `FieldState.required` directly and branch on `undefined`, or (b) rely on custom `ɵControl` components/native control attributes being re-written on every change detection cycle regardless of value change.

**Action:** none for typical usage; re-verify custom `ɵControl` components that expose `pattern` / `required` inputs (these are now always written, with `pattern: []` / `required: false` when unset, instead of being skipped), and code that checked `state.pattern === undefined` / `state.required === undefined` must check for the default empty array / `false` signal instead.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (ɵFieldState, packages/core/src/render3/interfaces/control.ts)
readonly pattern?: Signal<readonly RegExp[]>;
readonly required?: Signal<boolean>;

// After
readonly pattern: Signal<readonly RegExp[]>;
readonly required: Signal<boolean>;

// Before (FieldNode, packages/forms/signals/src/field/node.ts)
get pattern(): Signal<readonly RegExp[]> | undefined {
  return this.metadataOrUndefined(PATTERN);
}
get required(): Signal<boolean> | undefined {
  return this.metadataOrUndefined(REQUIRED);
}

// After
get pattern(): Signal<readonly RegExp[]> {
  return this.metadataOrUndefined(PATTERN) ?? EMPTY; // computed(() => [])
}
get required(): Signal<boolean> {
  return this.metadataOrUndefined(REQUIRED) ?? FALSE; // computed(() => false)
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 21.0.0 extends the signal-forms field-binding change detection optimization (originally PR https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64590, which only covered native/custom controls) to interoperable controls, i.e. `ControlValueAccessor`s used with signal `Field` on Reactive Forms (`@angular/forms/signals`). Previously `Field.ɵinteropControlUpdate()` unconditionally called `controlValueAccessor.writeValue(value)` and `setDisabledState?.(disabled)` (wrapped in `untracked`) on every update; now the core instruction `ɵɵcontrol` routes interop controls through a new `updateInteropControl` that compares `state.value()` and `state.disabled()` against the previous binding values (`controlBindingUpdated`) and only then calls `writeValue` / `setDisabledState` inside `untracked` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/850f0d6b3da734ec674a54829466af07f17b1982.patch, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/781a3299f9e16e16902f12f7e7c80c10f15f788a). As part of this, private APIs on the `ɵControl` interface in `@angular/core` changed: `ɵhasInteropControl`, `ɵinteropControlCreate()` and `ɵinteropControlUpdate()` were replaced by a single `ɵinteropControl: ɵInteropControl | undefined` property, with a new exported `ɵInteropControl` interface (`registerOnChange`, `registerOnTouched`, `writeValue`, optional `setDisabledState`) added to `@angular/core`'s private exports; `Field.ɵgetOrCreateNgControl()` became a `protected getOrCreateNgControl()` (patch above). This change is not listed as a breaking change in the 21.0.0 release notes (https://github.com/angular/angular/releases/tag/21.0.0).

**Affects:** Applications using signal forms (`Field` from `@angular/forms/signals`) bound to interoperable `ControlValueAccessor`-based / Reactive Forms controls; and any code that referenced the private `ɵhasInteropControl`, `ɵinteropControlCreate`, `ɵinteropControlUpdate` or `ɵgetOrCreateNgControl` members (custom `ɵControl` implementations).

**Action:** required: re-verify that two-way value/disabled propagation through `ControlValueAccessor`s bound with `field` still works (writes now only occur when the bound value or disabled state actually changed — custom CVAs that relied on `writeValue` being called every update may need adjustment). Any code implementing or calling the removed private `ɵControl` members (`ɵhasInteropControl`, `ɵinteropControlCreate()`, `ɵinteropControlUpdate()`, `Field.ɵgetOrCreateNgControl()`) must be updated to the new `ɵinteropControl` property / `ɵInteropControl` interface.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (Field, @angular/forms/signals)
get ɵhasInteropControl() { return this.controlValueAccessor !== undefined; }
ɵinteropControlUpdate() {
  const controlValueAccessor = this.controlValueAccessor!;
  const value = this.state().value();
  const disabled = this.state().disabled();
  untracked(() => {
    controlValueAccessor.writeValue(value);
    controlValueAccessor.setDisabledState?.(disabled);
  });
}

// After (ɵControl interface in @angular/core)
readonly ɵinteropControl: ɵInteropControl | undefined;
// updateInteropControl in core:
if (controlBindingUpdated(bindings, VALUE, value)) {
  untracked(() => interopControl.writeValue(value));
}
```

