**What changed:** A new optional, read-only `responseType` property (of the DOM's `ResponseType` union type) was added to `HttpResponseBase`, so it is available on both `HttpResponse` and `HttpErrorResponse`. It exposes the Fetch API `Response.type` value — `'basic'`, `'cors'`, `'opaque'`, `'opaqueredirect'`, or `'error'` — reflecting how the browser handled the request under CORS policy/request mode. It is populated only when HttpClient uses the Fetch-based backend configured via `withFetch()`; with the default XHR backend it is `undefined`. The `init` hash of `HttpResponse`, `HttpErrorResponse`, `HttpResponseBase` and `HttpResponse.clone()` now accepts `responseType?: ResponseType`, alongside the existing `redirected?: boolean` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63043, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/07e678872f91236f5c258f98a7aea536b5a200ac/packages/common/http/src/response.ts).

**Affects:** Applications using `HttpClient` with `provideHttpClient(withFetch())` that may read the new property; purely additive for all others. Custom subclasses or fakes of `HttpResponse`/`HttpErrorResponse` are unaffected since the property is optional.

**Action:** none — no migration step is required; optionally use the new property for CORS diagnostics when running on `withFetch()`.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// after (response.ts)
readonly responseType?: ResponseType;

// new usage
this.http.get('').subscribe({
  next: (res) => {
    if (responseType === 'opaque') {
      // no-cors: body/headers inaccessible
    }
  },
});
```

**What changed:** The `HttpClient` class is now annotated with `@Injectable({providedIn: 'root'})`, allowing it to be injected without calling the `provideHttpClient` provider function. Previously, `provideHttpClient` was required to configure `HttpClient` for injection. Now `HttpClient` can be used directly if default configuration (XSRF protection, fetch backend) is acceptable. The `provideHttpClient` function remains available for customizing HTTP interceptors, XSRF configuration, JSONP support, and backend selection (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/56212, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/4bed062bc9f2a0a66c9af3cb8aeb42ee023c6393, https://angular.dev/api/common/http/provideHttpClient).

**Affects:** Applications that inject `HttpClient` without calling `provideHttpClient` in their `bootstrapApplication` providers array.

**Action:** required: Re-verify that any application previously relying on `HttpClient` failing to inject (due to missing providers) does not now accidentally receive a default-configured instance. Applications requiring interceptors, custom XSRF, or specific backend options must still use `provideHttpClient` with the appropriate features (e.g., `withInterceptors`, `withFetch`).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before: HttpClient injection would fail without provideHttpClient
// bootstrap.ts
bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [
    // provideHttpClient was required here for HttpClient to be injectable
  ]
});

// After: HttpClient is injectable by default with default configuration
// bootstrap.ts
bootstrapApplication(AppComponent, {
  providers: [
    // HttpClient can now be injected without provideHttpClient
    // provideHttpClient is still needed if custom features like withInterceptors() are required
  ]
});
```

**What changed:** `HttpResponseBase.statusText` (property `readonly statusText: string` on `HttpResponseBase`, inherited by `HttpResponse` and `HttpErrorResponse`) is now marked `@deprecated` with the note: "With HTTP/2 and later versions, this will incorrectly remain set to 'OK' even when the status code of a response is not 200." The motivation: since HTTP/2, responses no longer carry a status text alongside the status code, so Angular's default value `'OK'` was being used in `HttpErrorResponse.message` (e.g. `Http failure response for <url>: 404 OK`) even for non-200 statuses (commit 0e4e17cd97; PR #64176). The deprecation itself changes no runtime behaviour in 21.0.0 — the property still exists and `HttpErrorResponse.message` still includes `init.statusText`; the message construction is only flagged internally with a TODO for later cleanup (commit patch).

**Affects:** Code that reads `.statusText` on `HttpResponse`/`HttpErrorResponse`, or that parses `HttpErrorResponse.message` expecting the trailing status text, plus builds that treat `@deprecated` usages as errors.

**Action:** required: stop relying on `statusText` / on the status text inside `HttpErrorResponse.message`; use the numeric `status` code instead. Re-verify any error-message string matching. (Property deprecation and rationale per commit patch; no removal in 21.0.0 shown.)

**Before/after:**
```ts
// @deprecated With HTTP/2 and later versions, this will incorrectly remain set to 'OK' even when the status code of a response is not 200.
readonly statusText: string;

// HttpErrorResponse message construction (unchanged in 21.0.0):
this.message = `Http failure response for ${init.url || '(unknown url)'}: ${init.status} ${
  init.statusText
}`;
```
(https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0e4e17cd97d7a5f7ccc40405ba2103a78e1e1298, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64176)

**What changed:** Angular 21 adds a schematics migration that replaces deprecated `RouterTestingModule` usages in test suites with `RouterModule`, and adds `provideLocationMocks()` when `Location` or `LocationStrategy` is imported and no custom providers exist (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64217). It is run via `ng g @angular/core:router-testing-migration` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/861cee34e0e9b5562cfe70d245f30b7ddea7d8fd). Example conversion shown in the PR: `imports: [RouterTestingModule.withRoutes(routes, { initialNavigation: 'enabledBlocking' })]` becomes `imports: [RouterModule.forRoot(routes, { initialNavigation: 'enabledBlocking' })]` (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64217). The PR is marked as not a breaking change (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64217).

**Affects:** Applications whose test files (`TestBed.configureTestingModule`) import or use `RouterTestingModule` from `@angular/router/testing`, especially those also importing `Location` or `LocationStrategy` from `@angular/common`.

**Action:** required: run the migration schematic `ng g @angular/core:router-testing-migration` after updating, then re-verify that routed test suites still configure routes and location mocking correctly (e.g. `initialNavigation` options are carried over and `provideLocationMocks()` is present where `Location`/`LocationStrategy` are used).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before
TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  imports: [RouterTestingModule.withRoutes(routes, { initialNavigation: 'enabledBlocking' })]
});

// After
TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  imports: [RouterModule.forRoot(routes, { initialNavigation: 'enabledBlocking' })],
});
```
(https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64217)

**What changed:** The built-in control flow migration (`packages/core/schematics/migrations/control-flow-migration/index.ts`), which runs as part of `ng update`, no longer throws when no migratable files are found. Previously it threw `SchematicsException('Could not find any files to migrate under the path .... Cannot run the control flow migration.')` per project, and also threw `'Could not find any tsconfig file. Cannot run the http providers migration.'` when no tsconfig was found; both are now `context.logger.warn(...)` calls — the latter becomes the warning `Could not find any tsconfig file. Cannot run the control flow migration.`, and when the total workspace-wide count of migratable source files is zero it logs `Control flow migration did not find any files to migrate`. The file count is now accumulated across all tsconfig files in the entire workspace rather than checked per project (`sourceFilesCount` summed over all `createMigrationProgram` results). One error remains: passing an `options.path` starting with `..` still throws `SchematicsException('Cannot run control flow migration outside of the current project.')` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/62bbce63b7abcb22f1fd453c59e0063aae3b189c, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64253).

**Affects:** Applications where `ng update` invokes the control flow migration (explicitly via `ng generate @angular/core:control-flow`, or automatically) and no templates/paths match — e.g. apps already migrated to the new control flow syntax or with a `path` option that matches no files.

**Action:** none — this only makes the migration non-fatal; previously failing updates now complete with a warning. Re-verify that `ng update` output warnings ("Control flow migration did not find any files to migrate") are expected rather than a missed migration.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (per project): threw and aborted ng update
if (sourceFiles.length === 0) {
  throw new SchematicsException(
    `Could not find any files to migrate under the path ${pathToMigrate}. Cannot run the control flow migration.`,
  );
}

// After: counts files workspace-wide and warns
} else if (sourceFilesCount === 0) {
  context.logger.warn('Control flow migration did not find any files to migrate');
}
```

**What changed:** The deprecated `ApplicationConfig` type alias export was removed from `@angular/platform-browser` (it had been `type ApplicationConfig = ApplicationConfigFromCore; export {ApplicationConfig};` in `packages/platform-browser/src/browser.ts`, also removed from `packages/platform-browser/src/platform-browser.ts` exports). `ApplicationConfig` must now be imported from `@angular/core` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ce8db665f984005264de0eb8b452370972823c17, https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63529). The PR is marked "detected: breaking change" for the major target (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63529). An automatic migration schematic was added: `application-config-core` in `packages/core/schematics/migrations.json` with `"version": "21.0.0"`, description "Moves imports of `ApplicationConfig` from `@angular/platform-browser` to `@angular/core`"; it rewrites imports, including aliased imports such as `ApplicationConfig as ApplicationConfigAlias` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ce8db665f984005264de0eb8b452370972823c17). Note `bootstrapApplication(rootComponent, options?: ApplicationConfig)` and `createApplication` remain in `@angular/platform-browser`; only the type export was removed.

**Affects:** Applications that import the `ApplicationConfig` type from `@angular/platform-browser` (e.g. to type a `bootstrapApplication`/`createApplication` config object).

**Action:** required: change imports of `ApplicationConfig` from `@angular/platform-browser` to `@angular/core` (aliased imports keep their alias). Alternatively run the included `ng update` migration schematic `application-config-core`, which does this automatically as part of the update to 21.0.0.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before
import { ApplicationConfig } from '@angular/platform-browser';
// after
import { ApplicationConfig } from '@angular/core';
```
(copied from the migration spec in https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ce8db665f984005264de0eb8b452370972823c17)

I have the full patch. One more quick check of the migration README is already included; the change is fully covered.

**What changed:** `Router.lastSuccessfulNavigation` changed from a plain getter returning `Navigation | null` to a getter returning `Signal<Navigation | null>`, so it must now be invoked as a signal call. The public API golden changed from `get lastSuccessfulNavigation(): Navigation | null;` to `get lastSuccessfulNavigation(): Signal<Navigation | null>;` (@https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/4e0fc81491bfe6e4eac5c59ef0bde908a3d58413). Internally, `lastSuccessfulNavigation` in `packages/router/src/navigation_transition.ts` became `signal<Navigation | null>(null)`. An `ng update` schematic migration `router-last-successful-navigation` (version `21.0.0`, "Ensures that the Router.lastSuccessfulNavigation signal is now invoked") rewrites property accesses `router.lastSuccessfulNavigation` into `router.lastSuccessfulNavigation()` for `Router` imported from `@angular/router` (@https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/63057, commit patch). Note: downstream packages relying on the old form (e.g. `@angular/ssr`) needed updating; an Angular SSR e2e test was disabled pending that (@https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/4e0fc81491bfe6e4eac5c59ef0bde908a3d58413).

**Affects:** Applications that read `Router.lastSuccessfulNavigation` (property access without invocation) in TypeScript files.

**Action:** required: run `ng update` so the `router-last-successful-navigation` migration converts reads to signal invocations; verify any code or third-party library (e.g. `@angular/ssr`) that accessed `lastSuccessfulNavigation` without invoking it is updated, since Angular's own SSR integration needed a corresponding update.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before
const navigation = this.router.lastSuccessfulNavigation;

// After
const navigation = this.router.lastSuccessfulNavigation();
```

**What changed:** Query parameters passed to `router.createUrlTree` were previously converted to strings via `` `${value}` ``, bypassing any custom `UrlSerializer` logic. Now the Router passes its injected `UrlSerializer` into `createUrlTreeFromSegmentGroup`, and each query param value is normalized by `normalizeQueryParams`: a temporary `UrlTree` is built with that param, then `urlSerializer.parse(urlSerializer.serialize(tree)).queryParams[k]` produces the value (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/64449, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/f6a73f19131b2befa74f4ea3f941038603958ac0). Arrays are still normalized element-by-element to preserve prior string-array behaviour. The exported helper `createUrlTreeFromSnapshot` gained a `urlSerializer = new DefaultUrlSerializer()` parameter; its docs state you should pass your application's custom `UrlSerializer` when one parses/serializes non-string query values. Fixes issue #47307, where objects in `queryParams` became `[object Object]` (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/47307). The PR is labeled `target: major, risky`.

**Affects:** Applications providing a custom `UrlSerializer` (`{provide: UrlSerializer, useClass: ...}`) that transforms query parameter values, and code calling `createUrlTreeFromSnapshot`; programmatic navigations with non-string `queryParams` values.

**Action:** required: re-verify that query param values read from `ActivatedRoute`/`UrlTree.queryParams` after `router.createUrlTree`/`navigate` now match the round-tripped (serialize+parse) form rather than the raw value passed in; when calling `createUrlTreeFromSnapshot` with a custom serializer configured, pass that serializer as the new 5th `urlSerializer` argument.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// old: values were just stringified in tree()
// new (packages/router/src/create_url_tree.ts):
function normalizeQueryParams(k: string, v: unknown, urlSerializer: UrlSerializer): unknown {
  k ||= 'ɵ';
  const tree = new UrlTree();
  tree.queryParams = {[k]: v};
  return urlSerializer.parse(urlSerializer.serialize(tree)).queryParams[k];
}

// createUrlTreeFromSnapshot now accepts the serializer:
createUrlTreeFromSnapshot(relativeTo, commands, queryParams, fragment,
  urlSerializer = new DefaultUrlSerializer());
```

**What changed:** The long-deprecated (since v5) dynamic hybrid bootstrapping API was deleted from `@angular/upgrade`: the `UpgradeAdapter` and `UpgradeAdapterRef` classes (previously exported from `@angular/upgrade`, i.e. `packages/upgrade/public_api.ts`, along with their source file `packages/upgrade/src/dynamic/src/upgrade_adapter.ts`) are no longer available at all in v18... specifically in 21.0.0 (commit f86846555b, PR #61659). Removed public methods included `UpgradeAdapter.bootstrap`, `downgradeNg2Component`, `downgradeNg2Provider`, `registerForNg1Tests`, `upgradeNg1Component`, `upgradeNg1Provider`, and `UpgradeAdapterRef.ready`/`dispose` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/f86846555bba44b2fb71d012fe4eebf82a0f5d00.patch). The `@angular/upgrade` package now only exports `VERSION` plus the `upgrade/static` APIs; the release note states: "`UpgradeAdapter` is no longer available. Use `upgrade/static` instead", which also supports AOT (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/61659).

**Affects:** Applications importing `UpgradeAdapter` or `UpgradeAdapterRef` from `@angular/upgrade` (the deprecated dynamic ngUpgrade API).

**Action:** required: replace all uses of `UpgradeAdapter`/`UpgradeAdapterRef` with the static API in `@angular/upgrade/static` (e.g. `downgradeComponent`/`downgradeInjectable`, which the deleted code itself delegated to, and the corresponding upgrade/static setup), and remove imports from `@angular/upgrade`. Apps not using these classes need no change.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (removed in v21; from the deleted playground example):
import {UpgradeAdapter} from '@angular/upgrade';

const adapter = new UpgradeAdapter(forwardRef(() => Ng2AppModule));
ng1module.directive('upgradeApp', adapter.downgradeNg2Component(UpgradeApp));
adapter.bootstrap(document.body, ['myExample']);

// After: use `upgrade/static` instead, which also supports AOT.
```
